1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,160
Prisoner's dilemma, right?

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:07,200
So the most commonly studied game in all of social science,

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:08,760
really, really without a doubt.

4
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:10,720
And again, with many applications.

5
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:12,140
Of course, when we apply it to the real world,

6
00:00:12,140 --> 00:00:14,720
then you'll notice that even when people appear

7
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:15,880
to be in a prisoner's dilemma,

8
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:18,160
it's actually pretty common for people to cooperate

9
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:19,720
to some degree.

10
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:21,760
There's not that many people who immediately stab you

11
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:23,080
in the back in a prisoner's dilemma,

12
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:24,920
although it has been known to happen.

13
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,640
And again, if you're actually thinking about

14
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:30,560
ever beating in a prisoner's dilemma,

15
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:32,400
you're wondering like the best way to play,

16
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,480
especially if it is even a finitely repeated

17
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:36,160
prisoner's dilemma.

18
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:39,040
In practice, you may be well advised to cooperate,

19
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:40,880
if only to create some confusion to your opponent

20
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:44,000
about whether you have a clue about what the game is about.

21
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,080
But we'll get to that later on.

22
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:48,120
All right, so prisoner's dilemma,

23
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:50,640
and now let's move on to coordination games.

24
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:53,240
Also very high profile, both theoretically

25
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:55,320
and also in policy.

26
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:56,880
All right, so here's a standard discussion.

27
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:59,000
We start with a game where both players

28
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,760
can either play left or right,

29
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:02,840
and then we say that if they both play left,

30
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:04,880
they both get three, if they both play right,

31
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:07,040
they both get five, and if they do different things,

32
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:08,600
then they both get zero.

33
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:10,320
That's the heart of the coordination game.

34
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:13,360
People do different things, then it's bad.

35
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:15,720
If they do the same things, then it's good,

36
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:18,400
but doing different same things,

37
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:20,980
some same things are better than other same things.

38
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:22,680
So in this one, both people playing right

39
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:25,200
is better for both of them than both playing left.

40
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:27,380
All right, and then this is a case

41
00:01:27,380 --> 00:01:29,660
where we very easily apply the concept

42
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:31,620
of pure strategy and actual equilibrium.

43
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:34,020
And again, it's three, three, a PS and E.

44
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:37,820
Is either person gained from switching unilaterally?

45
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:39,140
No, so that's one.

46
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:41,260
Is zero a zero, a PS and E?

47
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:43,500
No, no, no, that one.

48
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:45,220
What about five, five, is that a PS and E?

49
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:46,460
That one is two.

50
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:49,300
So here we've got a game where we've got two equilibrium.

51
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:51,780
So if you know the other person's playing left,

52
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:52,620
you wanna play left too.

53
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:53,940
If you know the other person's playing right,

54
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:56,500
you wanna play right too, right, and so on.

55
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:02,540
Now, coordination games are the basis

56
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:05,780
for the entire literature on so-called path dependence,

57
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:10,780
which really is just a fancy piece of vocabulary

58
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:12,760
for when it's a coordination game.

59
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:16,580
And again, the main idea that gets emphasized

60
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:21,060
in this area of path dependence is that it's possible,

61
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:24,220
underscore possible, that in a situation like this,

62
00:02:24,220 --> 00:02:27,020
you get locked into the bad equilibrium.

63
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:30,220
Not the really bad one, there's no danger of that,

64
00:02:30,220 --> 00:02:32,060
at least in the game, but there's a danger

65
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:34,900
of getting locked into the inferior equilibrium, right?

66
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:36,460
One that isn't the best possible one

67
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:38,180
from the point of view of anyone.

68
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:40,280
That can in fact happen.

69
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:42,180
Right, now remember, if you look at this,

70
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:43,340
is there anything about this game

71
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:46,340
that says that you naturally tend to go to this?

72
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:49,860
No, all right, so when people just say path dependence

73
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:51,740
and that's why things are terrible,

74
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:53,700
it's like, well, path dependence means

75
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:56,620
that things could be terrible, or at least,

76
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:59,380
maybe not so terrible you might think as those two.

77
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:01,620
And then we say mediocre might be a word

78
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:03,620
that you might apply, so path dependence

79
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:05,620
can lead to a mediocre outcome,

80
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:07,900
but it totally does not have to.

81
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:09,140
Totally does not have to.

82
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:13,380
Now, when you take a look at these two at Bolivia,

83
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:16,140
you have one thing, imagine you're playing this game

84
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:18,340
with one of the random students in this class, right?

85
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:20,180
So we both move simultaneously,

86
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:22,300
and your opponent plays left.

87
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:24,780
All right, what would your reaction be

88
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:27,380
if the person just played left out of the blue?

89
00:03:27,380 --> 00:03:29,540
I show you this game, you're paired up with a person

90
00:03:29,540 --> 00:03:31,980
and they play left, and then you say,

91
00:03:31,980 --> 00:03:33,460
wait, why in the world would you play left

92
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:35,220
if I thought you would play left?

93
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:38,700
Well, why would you think I would be so dumb as to play left?

94
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:41,540
All right, so there's something very odd

95
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:44,660
about starting this game from scratch

96
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:47,460
and ending up at that equilibrium, right?

97
00:03:47,460 --> 00:03:49,820
If you would be quite puzzled by the response

98
00:03:49,820 --> 00:03:51,700
of I just assumed you would play left,

99
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:53,860
so was the equilibrium, well, yeah,

100
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:55,940
but where would you ever get it in your head

101
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:57,500
that I would do left?

102
00:03:57,500 --> 00:03:58,580
Why?

103
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:00,100
Why would you even think that?

104
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:04,540
Now, in the real world, there may be cases

105
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:07,220
where it's easier to understand what's going on.

106
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:09,620
For example, suppose that we came up

107
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:12,180
with a better system for spelling in English.

108
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:15,300
How hard would it be to come up with a better system

109
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:17,460
for spelling in English?

110
00:04:17,460 --> 00:04:19,540
I think everyone in this room could improve

111
00:04:19,540 --> 00:04:22,700
English spelling given 30 minutes, right?

112
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:25,820
I mean, just think about the letters we should abolish.

113
00:04:25,820 --> 00:04:27,700
Q, there's no need for Q.

114
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:30,260
Q can be wiped off the face of the earth in English.

115
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:31,900
We never need it again.

116
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:34,140
Terrible letter, right?

117
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:36,460
Right, it's just a needless confusion, right?

118
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:38,580
You're like, so then you just go through,

119
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:41,140
like other letters, yeah, C, you don't need C.

120
00:04:41,140 --> 00:04:43,340
C is a useless letter, right?

121
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:46,980
And just go down the line, so it's X, you don't need X.

122
00:04:46,980 --> 00:04:48,660
So we got rid of three letters.

123
00:04:48,660 --> 00:04:49,900
We're like, this is what?

124
00:04:49,900 --> 00:04:54,340
This is like a 10% improvement in just about 30 seconds.

125
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:56,100
Like so, and then there's so many other things

126
00:04:56,100 --> 00:04:57,220
you need to fix.

127
00:04:57,220 --> 00:04:59,860
You know, so I cannot make all the spelling phonetic.

128
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:02,940
There are languages that work that way.

129
00:05:02,940 --> 00:05:05,180
Why can't English work that way?

130
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:07,540
All right, and of course, if you've had to learn English

131
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:09,260
as a second language, you know how terrible

132
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:10,940
our system is, right?

133
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:13,340
Isn't it a terrible system?

134
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:15,460
All right, and yet, does anyone feel inclined

135
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:18,780
to switch over to your new and improved spelling system?

136
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:20,020
Unilaterally?

137
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:21,820
Right, no one is going to try this.

138
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:23,900
People would think that you were crazy to do it.

139
00:05:23,900 --> 00:05:26,820
So it does seem like situations like that

140
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:29,980
can actually occur, but it's interesting to think about

141
00:05:29,980 --> 00:05:32,660
what's the difference between getting stuck

142
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:34,580
with a crummy spelling system

143
00:05:34,580 --> 00:05:37,420
and just doing a two by two game.

144
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:39,980
So like, the first one possibility is it's just

145
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:41,940
when there's more people, it's hard to switch.

146
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:44,940
Another one is when knowledge of what the better

147
00:05:44,940 --> 00:05:47,380
equilibrium is spreads gradually.

148
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:50,380
And so we'll talk about this more as we go on.

149
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:52,100
Now anyway, once you start thinking about

150
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:54,620
why this equilibrium seems to make so much more sense

151
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:56,820
than this one, it takes us very quickly

152
00:05:56,820 --> 00:05:59,740
to the notion of focal points,

153
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:02,980
also known as Schelling points.

154
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:07,780
Anyone get to see Thomas Schelling speak

155
00:06:07,780 --> 00:06:10,660
a year or two ago at the Public Choice Center?

156
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:13,300
So he died since then, but he was there.

157
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:14,860
He was so quiet I couldn't hear him actually,

158
00:06:14,860 --> 00:06:16,820
but people in the front row said he was great.

159
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:19,660
He was still very young, he was 96, I guess.

160
00:06:19,660 --> 00:06:21,660
Yes, it seems to me from what you're saying

161
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:24,460
that the idea of time dependence implies

162
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:27,340
or has this assumption of iteration, right?

163
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:31,500
Again, yeah, so you could say that, yeah,

164
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:35,180
because the idea is that since we are already expecting this

165
00:06:35,180 --> 00:06:37,300
then we'll keep doing this, so yeah.

166
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:40,020
But in a way what you're saying is that as an equilibrium

167
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:41,780
it's a repeated game, it's not.

168
00:06:41,780 --> 00:06:43,180
Yeah, it could be, so it could be,

169
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:45,820
although it could also be that it's just

170
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:48,740
people have expectations that the bad thing will occur

171
00:06:48,740 --> 00:06:50,980
and that just the expectations might lead to that

172
00:06:50,980 --> 00:06:52,900
even though there's no real repeat.

173
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:54,620
That's another possibility.

174
00:06:54,620 --> 00:06:55,460
Right.

175
00:06:55,460 --> 00:06:56,300
You could talk about time dependence,

176
00:06:56,300 --> 00:06:57,300
you know, one shot thing.

177
00:06:57,300 --> 00:06:58,620
Yeah, yeah, in principle.

178
00:06:58,620 --> 00:07:01,780
Again, like you might say that it's not truly one shot

179
00:07:01,780 --> 00:07:03,900
because everyone brings some expectations

180
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:06,300
about how people play games with them, right?

181
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:09,260
Which again sort of takes us to this idea here.

182
00:07:09,260 --> 00:07:10,540
So anyway, Thomas Schelling is famous

183
00:07:10,540 --> 00:07:12,140
for his idea of focal points.

184
00:07:12,140 --> 00:07:13,980
And he began with a case where he said,

185
00:07:13,980 --> 00:07:15,740
all right, look, suppose that tomorrow

186
00:07:15,740 --> 00:07:18,140
you have to meet an unknown person

187
00:07:18,140 --> 00:07:21,380
in somewhere in New York City at an unknown time.

188
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:23,620
All right, where and when do you go?

189
00:07:23,620 --> 00:07:26,100
And then he had everyone secretly write down their answer.

190
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:27,900
And he found out an extraordinarily high percentage

191
00:07:27,900 --> 00:07:29,800
of people would have successfully met.

192
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:34,540
All right, now if we were to go and write down

193
00:07:34,540 --> 00:07:36,900
this game in terms of this, there'd basically be

194
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:38,380
like four location.

195
00:07:38,380 --> 00:07:40,460
Essentially like the payoff is if you're at the same location

196
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:42,100
there's a good payoff.

197
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:44,580
And if you're not at the same location, there's a bad payoff.

198
00:07:44,580 --> 00:07:46,300
But then of course some locations are worse than others.

199
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:50,220
You could be in the worst neighborhood of New York City,

200
00:07:50,220 --> 00:07:51,260
for example.

201
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:52,700
Things wouldn't be that bad anymore

202
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:54,140
if that's what Schelling was writing.

203
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:55,060
It would be deadly.

204
00:07:57,060 --> 00:07:58,500
Anyway, so you think about it,

205
00:07:58,500 --> 00:07:59,980
you know, so like there's really an infinity

206
00:07:59,980 --> 00:08:00,820
of equilibrium, right?

207
00:08:00,820 --> 00:08:04,300
So like every location, like it's a continuous space.

208
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:06,620
And then of course time's continuous, right?

209
00:08:06,620 --> 00:08:07,460
Is time continuous?

210
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:08,500
It's continuous.

211
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:10,580
So when are people actually going,

212
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:11,900
when and where would people meet?

213
00:08:11,900 --> 00:08:14,860
And just to guess, when do you think people,

214
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:15,940
what time do you think people,

215
00:08:15,940 --> 00:08:17,540
almost everybody said they would meet?

216
00:08:17,540 --> 00:08:18,380
Noon.

217
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:19,200
Noon, right?

218
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:20,220
So noon pops in people's heads.

219
00:08:20,220 --> 00:08:21,980
Few people say midnight.

220
00:08:21,980 --> 00:08:23,440
But those are the two main ones.

221
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:26,700
And then where in New York City do you meet at noon?

222
00:08:26,700 --> 00:08:27,540
Town.

223
00:08:27,540 --> 00:08:28,660
Yes.

224
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:30,540
So that's one answer.

225
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:33,100
Another one is right into the clock at Grand Central Station

226
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:34,460
but the most popular one of all was

227
00:08:34,460 --> 00:08:36,500
the top of the Empire State Building.

228
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:37,500
I think this was before the movie

229
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:38,700
Sleepless in Seattle came out.

230
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:41,420
So it's already understood that there's something special

231
00:08:41,420 --> 00:08:44,260
about the top of the Empire State Building, right?

232
00:08:44,260 --> 00:08:47,100
And anyway, the neat thing about this is that

233
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:49,380
there's an infinity of equilibria

234
00:08:49,380 --> 00:08:51,260
and yet without any communication,

235
00:08:51,260 --> 00:08:55,220
there was in fact a lot of people were able to coordinate.

236
00:08:56,140 --> 00:08:57,540
And so there's something more focal

237
00:08:57,540 --> 00:09:00,020
about some equilibria rather than others.

238
00:09:00,020 --> 00:09:01,420
Right, now if you look at this one,

239
00:09:01,420 --> 00:09:02,500
knowing nothing else about it,

240
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:04,420
which one seems more focal to you?

241
00:09:04,420 --> 00:09:05,260
Right, right?

242
00:09:05,260 --> 00:09:07,100
Yeah, I mean, and it's nothing about like,

243
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:08,460
you know, society values,

244
00:09:08,460 --> 00:09:09,900
where you know, prefers right-handed people

245
00:09:09,900 --> 00:09:10,780
over left-handed people,

246
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:12,260
which is of course true too.

247
00:09:12,260 --> 00:09:13,100
Right.

248
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:13,940
I have to say.

249
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:14,780
At least when I was a kid,

250
00:09:14,780 --> 00:09:16,260
they were no longer making left-handed people

251
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:17,700
learn to write right-handed.

252
00:09:19,260 --> 00:09:21,380
But yeah, but there's just something about it.

253
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:22,740
Well, it's just a higher payout for both.

254
00:09:22,740 --> 00:09:24,580
So that could make it focal.

255
00:09:24,580 --> 00:09:26,700
But again, of course, if you live in a society

256
00:09:26,700 --> 00:09:29,660
where there's a superstition against the number five,

257
00:09:29,660 --> 00:09:31,100
then it might be, oh my God,

258
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:31,940
right in the middle of that,

259
00:09:31,940 --> 00:09:32,780
in the middle of the room in a building

260
00:09:32,780 --> 00:09:34,500
where there's no 13th floor.

261
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:36,140
Hmm, interesting.

262
00:09:36,140 --> 00:09:36,980
Like how do they,

263
00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:39,620
like how would you decide that though?

264
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:42,620
Given that there's people who avoid labeling floors

265
