01:13:10,000 --> 01:13:14,000
I mean a bunch of actual human beings competed.

217
01:13:14,000 --> 01:13:20,000
They broke their strategies down, and then there was a round robin where every strategy played every other strategy.

218
01:13:20,000 --> 01:13:23,000
And then we found a winner.

219
01:13:23,000 --> 01:13:29,000
So in the original tournament, the winning strategy in the repeated prisoner's dilemma was tit for tat,

220
01:13:29,000 --> 01:13:37,000
which means in turn one cooperate, and then in all subsequent turns do whatever your opponent did last turn.

221
01:13:37,000 --> 01:13:40,000
So notice, is tit for tat a trigger strategy?

222
01:13:40,000 --> 01:13:41,000
No.

223
01:13:41,000 --> 01:13:42,000
Not even close.

224
01:13:42,000 --> 01:13:46,000
Tit for tat just says if you go and betray me once, then I will betray you once.

225
01:13:46,000 --> 01:13:50,000
And if you get back and start cooperating again, then I'll get back and start cooperating again.

226
01:13:50,000 --> 01:13:56,000
And so tit for tat actually won, I think there was like 20 or 30 different strategies, and this one actually won.

