why did he murder his brother?

458
01:36:50,360 --> 01:36:52,960
Well, his brother had very close ties to the Chinese,

459
01:36:52,960 --> 01:36:55,880
which made his brother the absolute obvious pick

460
01:36:55,880 --> 01:36:58,400
for if the Chinese wanted to assassinate Kim Jong-un

461
01:36:58,400 --> 01:37:00,800
and replace him with someone else they could count on,

462
01:37:00,800 --> 01:37:02,260
that brother was perfect.

463
01:37:03,600 --> 01:37:04,760
And now he's dead.

464
01:37:04,760 --> 01:37:06,400
And now who's perfect?

465
01:37:06,400 --> 01:37:08,800
Now there's no perfect person to replace him.

466
01:37:08,800 --> 01:37:12,960
So, like, this is not only a message of the Chinese,

467
01:37:12,960 --> 01:37:14,640
but it builds up the reputation.

468
01:37:14,640 --> 01:37:17,360
Anyone who becomes the best person

469
01:37:17,360 --> 01:37:20,800
for the Chinese to replace me with dies.

470
01:37:20,800 --> 01:37:22,640
So now you have a country where we're saying,

471
01:37:22,640 --> 01:37:24,640
I'm not the best person to replace him with.

472
01:37:24,640 --> 01:37:26,520
No, no, no, I'm a terrible person.

473
01:37:26,520 --> 01:37:29,440
No, no, no, not me, not me, maybe that guy.

474
01:37:29,440 --> 01:37:30,840
Like, no, not me, don't look at me.

475
01:37:30,840 --> 01:37:33,520
I've got nothing to do with it.

476
01:37:33,520 --> 01:37:35,600
So, and this is, of course,

477
01:37:35,600 --> 01:37:37,400
very normal behavior for a dictator.

478
01:37:37,400 --> 01:37:42,400
Especially for, you know,

479
01:37:42,520 --> 01:37:44,560
for either, you know, like revolutionary dictators

480
01:37:44,560 --> 01:37:46,400
or people who had to fight their way to the top.

481
01:37:46,400 --> 01:37:48,720
For long-serving hereditary dictators

482
01:37:48,720 --> 01:37:50,720
where they have a very stable pattern of succession

483
01:37:50,720 --> 01:37:53,920
is less common because there's so much weight

484
01:37:53,920 --> 01:37:56,680
on the side of it has to be the legitimate heir.

485
01:37:56,680 --> 01:37:58,560
But, you know, like three generations of Kims

486
01:37:58,560 --> 01:38:00,560
isn't nearly enough to get that.

487
01:38:00,560 --> 01:38:01,640
So, you know, like, say,

488
01:38:01,640 --> 01:38:03,360
Gordon Tellock actually has this great story

489
01:38:03,360 --> 01:38:05,720
where he says that as long as you had to fight

490
01:38:05,720 --> 01:38:06,560
your way to the top,

491
01:38:06,560 --> 01:38:08,480
the people at the top are bloodthirsty.

492
01:38:08,480 --> 01:38:11,920
But once you get very secure hereditary succession,

493
01:38:11,920 --> 01:38:14,640
then the selection process gets weaker and weaker

494
01:38:14,640 --> 01:38:16,440
and finally you may get some wuss

495
01:38:16,440 --> 01:38:18,080
who's happy to switch to democracy

496
01:38:18,080 --> 01:38:19,320
if people like it for it.

497
01:38:20,440 --> 01:38:21,280
Yes?

498
01:38:21,280 --> 01:38:24,360
So in your view, regardless of the level of brutality,

499
01:38:24,360 --> 01:38:27,200
is predictability an asset or a liability?

500
01:38:28,280 --> 01:38:29,120
Let's see.

501
01:38:29,120 --> 01:38:31,120
If you're predictably brutal versus just,

502
01:38:31,120 --> 01:38:32,400
you looked at me funny.

503
01:38:32,400 --> 01:38:34,680
Yeah, so it's an interesting question.

504
01:38:34,680 --> 01:38:36,920
So like, many of the most successful dictators

505
01:38:36,920 --> 01:38:39,040
are like, you looked at me funny.

506
01:38:39,040 --> 01:38:41,400
Again, it seems like they're going for something saying,

507
01:38:41,400 --> 01:38:44,920
look, I will murder my best friend for no reason at all.

508
01:38:44,920 --> 01:38:47,040
So imagine what I would do to you.

509
01:38:48,320 --> 01:38:49,160
Right?

510
01:38:49,160 --> 01:38:50,680
And you might, if you were a good marginalist,

511
01:38:50,680 --> 01:38:52,360
say, well, that means the marginal cost

512
01:38:52,360 --> 01:38:54,760
of being your enemy is smaller than it seems.

513
01:38:54,760 --> 01:38:56,880
But there are enough dictators who do this

514
01:38:56,880 --> 01:38:57,800
and rule successfully,

515
01:38:57,800 --> 01:38:59,840
like, man, it seems like it is psychologically effective

516
01:38:59,840 --> 01:39:02,160
as to whether it's actually, you know,

517
01:39:02,160 --> 01:39:05,360
taken overboard by some of the most notorious dictators.

518
01:39:05,360 --> 01:39:07,720
I mean, I have to think that Stalin did take it overboard

519
01:39:07,720 --> 01:39:10,440
because he did things that were very strange,

520
01:39:10,440 --> 01:39:15,440
like he sent, you know, like his right-hand man,

521
01:39:15,720 --> 01:39:18,160
his Molotov, he sent the guy's wife to Siberia,

522
01:39:18,160 --> 01:39:21,120
he's a traitor, and keeps working with him every day.

523
01:39:22,680 --> 01:39:23,600
So.

524
01:39:23,600 --> 01:39:25,360
Maybe it was an unhappy marriage.

525
01:39:25,360 --> 01:39:27,360
You're excited about this, actually.

526
01:39:27,360 --> 01:39:29,600
Immediately, like, you know,

527
01:39:29,600 --> 01:39:31,120
what put you out as a Molotov repeatedly

528
01:39:31,120 --> 01:39:31,960
sort of broke the subject.

529
01:39:31,960 --> 01:39:34,840
Would you mind declaring my wife to not be a traitor

530
01:39:34,840 --> 01:39:36,120
and bring her back from Siberia?

531
01:39:36,120 --> 01:39:37,760
I kind of appreciate that.

532
01:39:37,760 --> 01:39:39,760
And then it's like, oh, maybe next year.

533
01:39:41,400 --> 01:39:42,240
So.

534
01:39:43,160 --> 01:39:44,000
Yeah.

535
01:39:44,000 --> 01:39:46,160
I mean, it's the kind of thing

536
01:39:46,160 --> 01:39:47,400
where it seems like you'd be worried

537
01:39:47,400 --> 01:39:49,120
that you'll push someone too far

538
01:39:49,120 --> 01:39:50,600
and they will just pick up a letter opener

539
01:39:50,600 --> 01:39:53,160
and kill you with it when they get a chance.

540
01:39:53,160 --> 01:39:54,640
But then again, of course,

541
01:39:54,640 --> 01:39:56,920
here there's no credible account

542
01:39:56,920 --> 01:40:00,000
of any remotely successful attempt


1
01:40:00,000 --> 01:40:01,360
of the most funstones like ever.

2
01:40:01,360 --> 01:40:02,860
Of course, for Hitler, there's some of the eight

3
01:40:02,860 --> 01:40:06,320
documented cases where people came pretty close to killing him.

4
01:40:06,320 --> 01:40:12,680
Because do ridiculous actions build kind of a god image?

5
01:40:12,680 --> 01:40:13,960
Yeah, possibly.

6
01:40:13,960 --> 01:40:16,160
So maybe that's what's going on.

7
01:40:16,160 --> 01:40:19,800
Maybe it's just that once you are that powerful dictator,

8
01:40:19,800 --> 01:40:21,320
you have a lot of wiggle room.

9
01:40:21,320 --> 01:40:23,960
And you can do something that is actually kind of foolish,

10
01:40:23,960 --> 01:40:26,760
but the odds that it actually leads to disaster for you

11
01:40:26,760 --> 01:40:29,600
is so low because you have so many other safeguards.

12
01:40:29,600 --> 01:40:31,880
So still, you might say, look, don't make enemies

13
01:40:31,880 --> 01:40:34,160
for no reason at all but for friends.

14
01:40:34,160 --> 01:40:36,480
That seems like pretty sensible advice,

15
01:40:36,480 --> 01:40:37,920
regardless of what's going on.

16
01:40:40,640 --> 01:40:42,800
All right, so anyway, so there is this intuition

17
01:40:42,800 --> 01:40:44,880
that repudiation does make things work better

18
01:40:44,880 --> 01:40:48,000
for all people, at least if all of them are participating.

19
01:40:48,000 --> 01:40:51,040
So how can you rationalize a standard intuition?

20
01:40:51,040 --> 01:40:52,400
So here, probably the best answer

21
01:40:52,400 --> 01:40:56,840
is that if you go and add on free entry and exit.

22
01:40:56,840 --> 01:41:00,040
So if you add on free entry and exit, then repudiation

23
01:41:00,040 --> 01:41:03,560
has the usual effects, or they were the expected effects.

24
01:41:03,560 --> 01:41:07,280
So free entry, free exit.

25
01:41:07,280 --> 01:41:08,880
That makes a big difference.

26
01:41:08,880 --> 01:41:11,360
All right, so then, if there's free entry and exit,

27
01:41:11,360 --> 01:41:13,280
and there's someone who has a bad reputation,

28
01:41:13,280 --> 01:41:16,400
what is everybody going to do?

29
01:41:16,400 --> 01:41:18,040
Leave, go away.

30
01:41:18,040 --> 01:41:20,880
So if there's only one place on Earth

31
01:41:20,880 --> 01:41:23,160
that you can buy food from, they might deliberately

32
01:41:23,160 --> 01:41:25,840
want to build up a reputation for we give you,

33
01:41:25,840 --> 01:41:29,000
we can charge exorbitant prices, we have crummy quality,

34
01:41:29,000 --> 01:41:31,440
and if you don't like it, we will cut you off from our list

35
01:41:31,440 --> 01:41:32,840
and you'll starve to death.

36
01:41:32,840 --> 01:41:34,840
That were the only place on Earth.

37
01:41:34,840 --> 01:41:38,120
But on the other hand, if there's

38
01:41:38,120 --> 01:41:40,800
other possibilities out there that you can constantly

39
01:41:40,800 --> 01:41:42,640
easily switch to, then of course,

40
01:41:42,640 --> 01:41:45,160
if you have that reputation, then you'll have zero customers.

41
01:41:45,160 --> 01:41:48,440
And that problem will solve itself.

42
01:41:48,440 --> 01:41:50,680
Again, of course, if, say, cities

43
01:41:50,680 --> 01:41:53,280
that are threatened by Genghis Khan could just pick themselves

44
01:41:53,280 --> 01:41:55,280
up and move 1,000 miles away, then again,

45
01:41:55,280 --> 01:41:58,200
Genghis Khan would not be doing anything

46
01:41:58,200 --> 01:41:59,760
that would work out well for him.

47
01:41:59,760 --> 01:42:01,600
It would just be pretty pointless.

48
01:42:01,600 --> 01:42:03,220
Now, Gordon Tullock, as far as I know,

49
01:42:03,220 --> 01:42:05,000
only did one experiment his whole career,

50
01:42:05,000 --> 01:42:07,160
but it was a brilliant experiment, one of the best

51
01:42:07,160 --> 01:42:08,200
ones I've ever heard of.

52
01:42:08,200 --> 01:42:10,160
So what Tullock did is this.

53
01:42:10,160 --> 01:42:12,640
He re-ran the standard prisoner of slum experiment

54
01:42:12,640 --> 01:42:14,880
where you get paired up with a partner and play,

55
01:42:14,880 --> 01:42:17,800
but he added on one difference from the rule,

56
01:42:17,800 --> 01:42:21,760
which is you're allowed to change your partner if you want.

57
01:42:21,760 --> 01:42:24,440
And the expected result happened.

58
01:42:24,440 --> 01:42:26,640
First of all, hardly anyone actually

59
01:42:26,640 --> 01:42:32,480
would betray anyone if they knew people could switch,

60
01:42:32,480 --> 01:42:33,200
for two reasons.

61
01:42:33,200 --> 01:42:34,640
So one is, if you did get betrayed,

62
01:42:34,640 --> 01:42:36,240
then you would almost always switch.

63
01:42:36,240 --> 01:42:38,120
Like, why should I stay with you after you're

64
01:42:38,120 --> 01:42:39,120
stabbed me in the back?

65
01:42:39,120 --> 01:42:41,960
And then secondly, once you have betrayed someone,

66
01:42:41,960 --> 01:42:44,300
who are the only people that are willing to have anything

67
01:42:44,300 --> 01:42:46,440
to do with you?

68
01:42:46,440 --> 01:42:48,000
Other betrayers.

69
01:42:48,000 --> 01:42:50,040
And it seemed like players figured this out

70
01:42:50,040 --> 01:42:50,680
pretty quickly.

71
01:42:50,680 --> 01:42:53,560
So even people who were inclined to betray would say, oh, wait.

72
01:42:53,560 --> 01:42:54,400
I've got to go.

73
01:42:54,400 --> 01:42:56,920
If I betray, not only am I going to lose my partner,

74
01:42:56,920 --> 01:42:59,640
I'm going to be thrown into this crummy,

75
01:42:59,640 --> 01:43:02,480
you know, this crummy, lupine proletariat of people

76
01:43:02,480 --> 01:43:05,280
who no one else wants to have anything to do with.

77
01:43:05,280 --> 01:43:06,880
I'll be ostracized.

78
01:43:06,880 --> 01:43:08,120
I'll be an outcast.

79
01:43:08,120 --> 01:43:10,840
And then I'll only be able to deal with other people like me.

80
01:43:10,840 --> 01:43:12,520
People like me are terrible.

81
01:43:12,520 --> 01:43:14,800
So I don't want that.

82
01:43:14,800 --> 01:43:17,040
So Tullock set this up in this experiment.

83
01:43:17,040 --> 01:43:19,720
This was a hypothesis, and it worked great.

84
01:43:19,720 --> 01:43:21,880
So really a pretty amazing experiment,

85
01:43:21,880 --> 01:43:26,160
showing that you could get near total cooperation just

86
01:43:26,160 --> 01:43:28,480
by making it known that if you were not

87
01:43:28,480 --> 01:43:30,840
happy with the result, you could go and fire your partner.

88
01:43:33,080 --> 01:43:34,640
You may have had this in mind any time

89
01:43:34,640 --> 01:43:36,680
that you were stuck on a class assignment

90
01:43:36,680 --> 01:43:39,720
and the teacher just forcibly crammed people together.

91
01:43:39,720 --> 01:43:41,920
And then, of course, there's always a bunch of people

92
01:43:41,920 --> 01:43:42,840
who don't do any work, and then they

93
01:43:42,840 --> 01:43:44,500
get shoved off onto people who actually

94
01:43:44,500 --> 01:43:46,000
want to get a good grade.

95
01:43:46,000 --> 01:43:48,720
You may have noticed this occurring at some point.

96
01:43:48,720 --> 01:43:51,360
But on the other hand, if you were free to form your groups,

97
01:43:51,360 --> 01:43:53,280
then it probably wouldn't happen.

98
01:43:53,280 --> 01:43:56,560
So my kids told me the usual thing.

99
01:43:56,560 --> 01:43:58,160
Teachers actually, if you suggest,

100
01:43:58,160 --> 01:43:59,720
will let people form their own groups.

101
01:43:59,720 --> 01:44:00,440
They'll say, well, then it'll just

102
01:44:00,440 --> 01:44:02,440
be some groups of hardworking kids

103
01:44:02,440 --> 01:44:05,240
and other groups of lazy kids, and we don't want that.

104
01:44:05,240 --> 01:44:07,160
Isn't that how it ought to be?

105
01:44:07,160 --> 01:44:09,560
Isn't that a well-functioning system

106
01:44:09,560 --> 01:44:12,160
where people who don't do work are stuck with other people who

107
01:44:12,160 --> 01:44:13,240
don't do work?

108
01:44:13,240 --> 01:44:15,440
And if you want to turn over a new leaf,

109
01:44:15,440 --> 01:44:18,880
you have to go and prove yourself and pay your dues

110
01:44:18,880 --> 01:44:23,080
and earn forgiveness for your past laziness.

111
01:44:23,080 --> 01:44:24,440
No, that's not a good system.

112
01:44:24,440 --> 01:44:26,200
A good system is where every good kid

113
01:44:26,200 --> 01:44:27,600
is shackled to some freeloaders.

114
01:44:27,600 --> 01:44:31,320
All right.

115
01:44:31,320 --> 01:44:34,400
God, I hate class projects so much.

116
01:44:34,400 --> 01:44:37,720
The model of absolute injustice.

117
01:44:37,720 --> 01:44:38,640
So questions.

118
01:44:38,640 --> 01:44:40,840
Questions about the Talk experiment,

119
01:44:40,840 --> 01:44:42,240
reputation in general.

120
01:44:42,240 --> 01:44:44,040
And again, if you do want to work on anything

121
01:44:44,040 --> 01:44:46,840
involving reputation, you are already in the right place.

122
01:44:46,840 --> 01:44:50,360
So Dan Klein's a great person to talk to.

123
01:44:50,360 --> 01:44:54,240
Anyone who's doing experiments is a great person to talk to.

124
01:44:54,240 --> 01:44:57,080
So I guess the main issue is trying

125
01:44:57,080 --> 01:45:00,880
to go and get credit for doing something not obvious.

126
01:45:00,880 --> 01:45:03,200
Because of course, you can't write a paper on something

127
01:45:03,200 --> 01:45:05,120
obvious is the problem.

128
01:45:05,120 --> 01:45:07,280
Dan Klein wrote about it before it happened,

129
01:45:07,280 --> 01:45:09,120
which I think made it less obvious.

130
01:45:09,120 --> 01:45:11,040
So there's that.

131
01:45:11,040 --> 01:45:13,040
But again, I know he's got a bunch of other ideas

132
01:45:13,040 --> 01:45:14,760
about how reputation can be expended.

133
01:45:14,760 --> 01:45:18,480
So there's probably plenty of other things to do with it.

134
01:45:18,480 --> 01:45:18,980
All right.

135
01:45:18,980 --> 01:45:20,560
So questions about that.

136
01:45:20,560 --> 01:45:22,880
We have had some GMU students who've done dissertations

137
01:45:22,880 --> 01:45:25,000
on the dark web and things like that.

138
01:45:25,000 --> 01:45:28,800
And how can you have reputation with anonymity?

139
01:45:28,800 --> 01:45:31,680
Which at first glance sounds impossible.

140
01:45:31,680 --> 01:45:36,360
So my student, Julia Norgaard, had one on the dark web.

141
01:45:36,360 --> 01:45:40,400
And again, I have to say, I've never actually been personally

142
01:45:40,400 --> 01:45:41,880
on the dark web.

143
01:45:41,880 --> 01:45:46,920
But her story basically was that even though the identity

144
01:45:46,920 --> 01:45:49,360
of a human being is anonymous, but still there

145
01:45:49,360 --> 01:45:52,760
is a reputation of this anonymous entity.

146
01:45:52,760 --> 01:45:55,680
So there's like the anonymous store number one.

147
01:45:55,680 --> 01:45:57,400
And you know that you have previously

148
01:45:57,400 --> 01:45:59,520
had good relations with anonymous store number one.

149
01:45:59,520 --> 01:46:03,740
And so that is where you get the benefit.

150
01:46:03,740 --> 01:46:05,780
So I do remember that when I was in grad school,

151
01:46:05,780 --> 01:46:08,560
I had a bunch of arguments over email with my econ one teacher.

152
01:46:08,560 --> 01:46:09,760
I said, well, I'm a graduate.

153
01:46:09,760 --> 01:46:11,200
I said, my undergraduate econ one teacher

154
01:46:11,200 --> 01:46:12,960
had a bunch of arguments with him over email.

155
01:46:12,960 --> 01:46:14,120
Well, I kept saying the reputation

156
01:46:14,120 --> 01:46:15,360
to do way more things than he thought.

157
01:46:15,360 --> 01:46:17,840
And he kept saying that it was just libertarian fantasy.

158
01:46:17,840 --> 01:46:21,600
And then in the early 2000s, he spontaneously

159
01:46:21,600 --> 01:46:24,760
remembered the conversation, remembered

160
01:46:24,760 --> 01:46:28,040
our respective positions, and set out his right.

161
01:46:28,040 --> 01:46:31,240
Which is like being struck by lightning, right?

162
01:46:31,240 --> 01:46:33,160
So normally the best you can hope for

163
01:46:33,160 --> 01:46:34,600
is that someone changed their mind

164
01:46:34,600 --> 01:46:38,280
without remembering the users of credit for it.

165
01:46:38,280 --> 01:46:41,440
And he said, look, I just thought that the whole asymmetric

166
01:46:41,440 --> 01:46:43,000
information problem would kill this.

167
01:46:43,000 --> 01:46:45,280
I never believed online commerce could take off.

168
01:46:45,280 --> 01:46:47,240
And now it has.

169
01:46:47,240 --> 01:46:50,840
And if you think about what one kind of business

170
01:46:50,840 --> 01:46:53,120
has been absolutely instrumental to the internet working

171
01:46:53,120 --> 01:46:55,760
as it has, so like has anyone ever bought anything

172
01:46:55,760 --> 01:46:58,840
with cash over the internet ever?

173
01:46:58,840 --> 01:46:59,640
You never use cash.

174
01:46:59,640 --> 01:47:02,280
So what has been the key institution that has

175
01:47:02,280 --> 01:47:05,040
allowed the system to flourish?

176
01:47:05,040 --> 01:47:05,540
Credit.

177
01:47:05,540 --> 01:47:06,320
Credit cards.

