01:47:06,320 --> 01:47:07,640
Credit cards are crucial.

179
01:47:07,640 --> 01:47:10,920
Even PayPal ultimately comes back to credit cards, right?

180
01:47:10,920 --> 01:47:13,320
So credit cards have been crucial.

181
01:47:13,320 --> 01:47:15,400
If you think about how much the modern world owes

182
01:47:15,400 --> 01:47:18,720
to credit cards, not just all e-commerce,

183
01:47:18,720 --> 01:47:23,240
but like rental cars, and like hotels,

184
01:47:23,240 --> 01:47:25,640
and just go down the list of all the things

185
01:47:25,640 --> 01:47:27,520
you can do with a credit card.

186
01:47:27,520 --> 01:47:29,000
Mail order, right?

187
01:47:29,000 --> 01:47:31,160
And again, what credit cards are all about,

188
01:47:31,160 --> 01:47:33,160
I mean, even in the name credit card,

189
01:47:33,160 --> 01:47:35,440
what does the word credit suggest?

190
01:47:35,440 --> 01:47:36,080
You're reliable.

191
01:47:36,080 --> 01:47:37,760
Yeah, yes, we're reliable.

192
01:47:37,760 --> 01:47:39,520
We have a reputation.

193
01:47:39,520 --> 01:47:43,040
So you have a reputation as someone that repays his debts.

194
01:47:43,040 --> 01:47:45,240
And pretty amazing given that it's essentially

195
01:47:45,240 --> 01:47:47,740
possible for you to rack up an enormous amount of credit card

196
01:47:47,740 --> 01:47:49,280
debt and then just declare bankruptcy

197
01:47:49,280 --> 01:47:51,160
and not have to pay it.

198
01:47:51,160 --> 01:47:55,200
So fun fact is that this is doable.

199
01:47:55,200 --> 01:47:57,680
It's also illegal to go and sign a contract waiving

200
01:47:57,680 --> 01:47:59,200
your right to bankruptcy.

201
01:47:59,200 --> 01:48:00,720
So imagine what kind of a great deal

202
01:48:00,720 --> 01:48:02,520
you would get from credit card companies

203
01:48:02,520 --> 01:48:06,060
if you would just waive that right.

204
01:48:06,060 --> 01:48:07,560
So questions about reputation?

205
01:48:07,560 --> 01:48:08,720
Everyone happy?

206
01:48:08,720 --> 01:48:10,040
Inspired?

207
01:48:10,040 --> 01:48:10,540
Great.

208
01:48:10,540 --> 01:48:11,040
All right.

209
01:48:11,040 --> 01:48:12,920
So let's all take out our credit cards

210
01:48:12,920 --> 01:48:17,360
and say a silent prayer of thanks for all that we owe them

211
01:48:17,360 --> 01:48:19,320
and all that they have done for us,

212
01:48:19,320 --> 01:48:22,560
and how rarely anyone has ever written a poem

213
01:48:22,560 --> 01:48:26,720
to the noble credit card, how rarely anyone has ever held it

214
01:48:26,720 --> 01:48:28,760
up and cried a tear.

215
01:48:28,760 --> 01:48:30,320
Said, it was you.

216
01:48:30,320 --> 01:48:33,600
I love you and all you've done for us.

217
01:48:33,600 --> 01:48:36,440
There had never been a motion in the United Nations saying,

218
01:48:36,440 --> 01:48:39,600
let us all have a day of honor for credit cards.

219
01:48:39,600 --> 01:48:42,200
But should there be?

220
01:48:42,200 --> 01:48:45,120
Can't we boldly remember the real heroes, credit card

221
01:48:45,120 --> 01:48:45,620
companies?

222
01:48:45,620 --> 01:48:48,000
All right.

223
01:48:48,000 --> 01:48:49,000
All right.

224
01:48:49,000 --> 01:48:50,280
So then moving on.

225
01:48:50,280 --> 01:48:53,760
All right, so now we got monopoly and congestibility.

226
01:48:53,760 --> 01:48:54,520
All right.

227
01:48:54,520 --> 01:48:57,480
So you've all seen the standard monopoly model at some point,

228
01:48:57,480 --> 01:48:58,360
right?

229
01:48:58,360 --> 01:49:00,160
Standard monopoly model.

230
01:49:00,160 --> 01:49:03,040
So what does the monopolist do?

231
01:49:03,040 --> 01:49:04,080
You all know the answer.

232
01:49:04,080 --> 01:49:08,320
What the monopolist does is they maximize PQ minus TC.

233
01:49:08,320 --> 01:49:12,800
All right, so price times quantity minus total cost.

234
01:49:12,800 --> 01:49:15,000
And when they do so, they wind up

235
01:49:15,000 --> 01:49:17,640
setting marginal revenue equal to marginal cost.

236
01:49:17,640 --> 01:49:20,400
So if this is demand curve, then this

237
01:49:20,400 --> 01:49:22,400
will be your marginal revenue curve.

238
01:49:22,400 --> 01:49:25,120
And if this is your marginal cost curve,

239
01:49:25,120 --> 01:49:28,440
then monopolists, of course, have we seen 1,000 times,

240
01:49:28,440 --> 01:49:30,880
just go to the intersection of marginal cost and marginal

241
01:49:30,880 --> 01:49:34,360
revenue, and then you move up to the demand curve.

242
01:49:34,360 --> 01:49:37,000
And then that tells you what they will do.

243
01:49:37,000 --> 01:49:40,480
So you've all seen what monopolists do before.

244
01:49:40,480 --> 01:49:41,400
OK.

245
01:49:41,400 --> 01:49:45,480
Now, does this make sense in game theoretic terms?

246
01:49:45,480 --> 01:49:48,480
Well, sure, this is one equilibrium.

247
01:49:48,480 --> 01:49:50,320
It's totally one equilibrium.

248
01:49:50,320 --> 01:49:52,600
But there's another equilibrium for you.

249
01:49:52,600 --> 01:49:55,280
How about an equilibrium where consumers refuse to pay anything

250
01:49:55,280 --> 01:49:57,320
more than marginal cost, and then monopolists

251
01:49:57,320 --> 01:49:59,680
set price at marginal cost?

252
01:49:59,680 --> 01:50:02,240
If this is what everybody believes is going to happen,

253
01:50:02,240 --> 01:50:03,840
does anyone have a reason to switch?

254
01:50:03,840 --> 01:50:06,160
If the monopolist believes that no one will buy anything

255
01:50:06,160 --> 01:50:08,360
at more than marginal cost, it has no reason

256
01:50:08,360 --> 01:50:10,040
to charge more than marginal cost.

257
01:50:10,040 --> 01:50:15,520
And if consumers refuse to pay anything

258
01:50:15,520 --> 01:50:17,760
more than marginal cost, and they're

259
01:50:17,760 --> 01:50:20,260
getting at a marginal cost, do they have any reason to offer

260
01:50:20,260 --> 01:50:20,760
more?

261
01:50:20,760 --> 01:50:21,520
No.

262
01:50:21,520 --> 01:50:23,680
So we actually have another equilibrium

263
01:50:23,680 --> 01:50:25,120
at the other extreme.

264
01:50:25,120 --> 01:50:28,280
That's also totally an equilibrium.

265
01:50:28,280 --> 01:50:30,200
So then you would sit back and say, all right,

266
01:50:30,200 --> 01:50:31,600
well, what extra assumptions do we

267
01:50:31,600 --> 01:50:34,160
need in order for this to make sense?

268
01:50:34,160 --> 01:50:37,000
So one thing you might do is, what

269
01:50:37,000 --> 01:50:40,280
would be a sub-game perfection, where, sure, the customer's

270
01:50:40,280 --> 01:50:41,760
going to say we won't pay anything more than marginal

271
01:50:41,760 --> 01:50:42,280
cost.

272
01:50:42,280 --> 01:50:43,920
But when you show up at a store and they're

273
01:50:43,920 --> 01:50:45,280
charging more than marginal cost,

274
01:50:45,280 --> 01:50:47,640
what does the monopolist think you're really going to do?

275
01:50:47,640 --> 01:50:48,280
Buy it.

276
01:50:48,280 --> 01:50:49,440
Buy it.

277
01:50:49,440 --> 01:50:52,280
So you could say, really, sub-game perfection

278
01:50:52,280 --> 01:50:55,560
is built into the standard monopoly model, a concept

279
01:50:55,560 --> 01:50:57,760
that you may have never heard of until last week.

280
01:50:57,760 --> 01:51:01,200
Actually underlies the whole ordinary store monopoly.

281
01:51:01,200 --> 01:51:03,760
Or you might actually say maybe what's really

282
01:51:03,760 --> 01:51:05,320
going on is a reputational model,

283
01:51:05,320 --> 01:51:08,080
where the monopolist is trying to build up a reputation

284
01:51:08,080 --> 01:51:11,000
for charging the monopoly price.

285
01:51:11,000 --> 01:51:12,720
And it's hard, on the other hand,

286
01:51:12,720 --> 01:51:16,120
for the customers to build up a reputation for not doing that.

287
01:51:16,120 --> 01:51:17,920
So customers say, come on, you guys.

288
01:51:17,920 --> 01:51:19,800
Let's all build up a reputation for not

289
01:51:19,800 --> 01:51:22,240
being willing to go along with these store prices.

290
01:51:22,240 --> 01:51:24,520
But of course, every individual is likely to say, well,

291
01:51:24,520 --> 01:51:26,960
I'd like to go and eat some chicken this weekend.

292
01:51:26,960 --> 01:51:28,640
If I just go and pay the monopoly price,

293
01:51:28,640 --> 01:51:29,760
I can get chicken.

294
01:51:29,760 --> 01:51:31,560
Whereas the monopolist, on the other hand,

295
01:51:31,560 --> 01:51:33,760
doesn't have to go and get a whole group of people

296
01:51:33,760 --> 01:51:35,400
together to go along with this plan.

297
01:51:35,400 --> 01:51:37,560
Just says, look, this is what I'm doing.

298
01:51:37,560 --> 01:51:40,440
And every week, I charge this price for chickens.

299
01:51:40,440 --> 01:51:42,080
I've been doing this for a long time.

300
01:51:42,080 --> 01:51:43,880
So if you think I'm going to cut the price,

301
01:51:43,880 --> 01:51:45,280
you're sadly mistaken, people.

302
01:51:45,280 --> 01:51:47,720
So that might be it.

303
01:51:47,720 --> 01:51:50,680
So in any case, the simple monopoly model

304
01:51:50,680 --> 01:51:53,800
actually needs better intellectual foundations

305
01:51:53,800 --> 01:51:56,960
than your intro textbook gives it.

306
01:51:56,960 --> 01:51:59,840
But of course, that doesn't mean that it's wrong.

307
01:51:59,840 --> 01:52:02,160
So it means that, yeah, so you need extra foundations.

308
01:52:02,160 --> 01:52:04,720
But you just figure out whatever foundations are necessary

309
01:52:04,720 --> 01:52:06,080
to make the standard story right,

310
01:52:06,080 --> 01:52:08,560
because the standard story does make sense.

311
01:52:08,560 --> 01:52:11,440
So intuitively, the standard story makes a lot of sense.

312
01:52:11,440 --> 01:52:14,480
Again, especially if we are thinking about the simplest

313
01:52:14,480 --> 01:52:17,400
kind of monopoly, which is where the government goes

314
01:52:17,400 --> 01:52:19,680
into an industry where there's no real reason for there

315
01:52:19,680 --> 01:52:20,360
to be a monopoly.

316
01:52:20,360 --> 01:52:22,760
And then they give a piece of paper to one firm that says,

317
01:52:22,760 --> 01:52:24,680
you are the only one allowed to produce this.

318
01:52:24,680 --> 01:52:27,560
And then they jail anyone who tries to meet with them.

319
01:52:27,560 --> 01:52:29,760
So that is the simplest case.

320
01:52:29,760 --> 01:52:32,880
Notice here how I drew a flat marginal cost curve.

321
01:52:32,880 --> 01:52:34,280
In the real world, how often do you

322
01:52:34,280 --> 01:52:36,160
get a monopolist in an industry where there's

323
01:52:36,160 --> 01:52:38,320
a flat marginal cost curve?

324
01:52:38,320 --> 01:52:40,840
Pretty much never, right?

325
01:52:40,840 --> 01:52:44,560
So instead, the only way you get monopoly

326
01:52:44,560 --> 01:52:46,480
in an industry with a flat marginal cost curve

327
01:52:46,480 --> 01:52:49,520
is something like agri-oil, like when their government organized

328
01:52:49,520 --> 01:52:50,840
agricultural cartels.

329
01:52:50,840 --> 01:52:53,000
Again, why did the government organize the cartels?

330
01:52:53,000 --> 01:52:54,960
Well, because there's very flat marginal cost.

331
01:52:54,960 --> 01:52:57,520
So if the government just looked the other way

332
01:52:57,520 --> 01:53:00,360
while the firm is trying to collude, it falls through.

333
01:53:00,360 --> 01:53:02,760
It's too easy for there to be more entry,

334
01:53:02,760 --> 01:53:06,000
too easy for one old apple to gain production.

335
01:53:06,000 --> 01:53:07,440
So that's not likely to work.

336
01:53:07,440 --> 01:53:10,120
But anyway, if we think about the simple case where

337
01:53:10,120 --> 01:53:12,680
it is a government-sponsored monopoly, where there's

338
01:53:12,680 --> 01:53:15,640
no possible economic reason for it to exist,

339
01:53:15,640 --> 01:53:18,160
other than the government wants to go and help out

340
01:53:18,160 --> 01:53:21,000
the bully in the dictator, something like that.

341
01:53:21,000 --> 01:53:23,080
Anyway, so in that very simple case,

342
01:53:23,080 --> 01:53:25,640
this seems like a fine model of what's likely to happen.

343
01:53:29,760 --> 01:53:32,960
But for all other cases, when it's not just government

344
01:53:32,960 --> 01:53:34,800
going and handing out a piece of paper saying,

345
01:53:34,800 --> 01:53:36,920
only you can legally prove something,

346
01:53:36,920 --> 01:53:38,960
then there is a big real-world problem,

347
01:53:38,960 --> 01:53:41,320
which is the problem of potential competition.

348
01:53:45,480 --> 01:53:47,440
Something can literally be a monopoly

349
01:53:47,440 --> 01:53:49,040
where there's only one seller, right?

350
01:53:49,040 --> 01:53:51,680
Like some mono meaning one, poly meaning seller,

351
01:53:51,680 --> 01:53:53,800
or something like that.

352
01:53:53,800 --> 01:53:55,200
Something like polis or something.

353
01:53:55,200 --> 01:53:56,880
So that's the original word.

354
01:53:56,880 --> 01:53:59,320
There can literally only be one seller of something.

355
01:53:59,320 --> 01:54:02,320
And yet, they might actually act in a way

356
01:54:02,320 --> 01:54:05,120
that's very different from this if they are worried

357
01:54:05,120 --> 01:54:07,280
that they won't remain the only firm if they

358
01:54:07,280 --> 01:54:09,040
change their behavior.

359
01:54:09,040 --> 01:54:11,560
And it's a pretty simple point.

360
01:54:11,560 --> 01:54:14,960
So how can we go and model potential competition?

361
01:54:14,960 --> 01:54:18,240
How can we go and amend the regular monopoly model

362
01:54:18,240 --> 01:54:22,800
to take into account the possibility of potential entry

363
01:54:22,800 --> 01:54:25,200
or potential competition?

364
01:54:25,200 --> 01:54:28,080
By the way, again, this is something where, like in 1990,

365
01:54:28,080 --> 01:54:30,240
you said this thing, a lot of people would still say,

366
01:54:30,240 --> 01:54:32,960
ah, come on, potential competition, no.

367
01:54:32,960 --> 01:54:35,440
In the internet age, I think it's almost crazy

368
01:54:35,440 --> 01:54:38,000
to go and dismiss potential competition

369
01:54:38,000 --> 01:54:39,800
because if you don't believe in that,

370
01:54:39,800 --> 01:54:42,840
what's your story for why Amazon doesn't raise prices?

371
01:54:42,840 --> 01:54:46,520
We think that Amazon would remain the only Amazon

372
01:54:46,520 --> 01:54:47,640
if they doubled its prices.

373
01:54:47,640 --> 01:54:49,480
Why would they double them?

374
01:54:49,480 --> 01:54:53,240
We really think the demand elasticity is that high

375
01:54:53,240 --> 01:54:55,680
that Amazon would actually lose money if they doubled

376
01:54:55,680 --> 01:54:57,840
their prices today.

377
01:54:57,840 --> 01:54:59,960
Seems like they could totally get away with it.

378
01:54:59,960 --> 01:55:01,560
And there's plenty of other firms

379
01:55:01,560 --> 01:55:03,480
that basically work the same way, right?

380
01:55:03,480 --> 01:55:06,120
Plenty of other famous internet firms like Netflix.

381
01:55:06,120 --> 01:55:09,720
So if Netflix were to suddenly greatly increase its prices,

382
01:55:09,720 --> 01:55:12,320
that's why I'm not quite as sure that they don't have high demand

383
01:55:12,320 --> 01:55:12,800
elasticity.

384
01:55:12,800 --> 01:55:15,640
But it sort of seems like they could get away with more.

385
01:55:15,640 --> 01:55:19,960
But Netflix probably is worried about making more Netflixes.

386
01:55:19,960 --> 01:55:22,280
They don't want there to be other competitors.

387
01:55:22,280 --> 01:55:24,880
And that is a reason to constantly be producing

388
01:55:24,880 --> 01:55:29,120
more stuff and to keep prices at a reasonable level

389
01:55:29,120 --> 01:55:31,680
so that customers are so happy they aren't looking around

390
01:55:31,680 --> 01:55:32,920
for anything else.

391
01:55:32,920 --> 01:55:34,920
So basically, offer a better feel

392
01:55:34,920 --> 01:55:37,880
than anyone else on Earth thinks they can make a profit at.

393
01:55:37,880 --> 01:55:41,920
And then you've got a winning business model.

394
01:55:41,920 --> 01:55:44,240
All right, so what can we do?

395
01:55:44,240 --> 01:55:46,000
Well, there's several different approaches

396
01:55:46,000 --> 01:55:47,320
to potential competition.

397
01:55:47,320 --> 01:55:50,440
Let's start with contestability.

398
01:55:56,760 --> 01:56:00,880
Contestability was a model that became big during the late 70s

399
01:56:00,880 --> 01:56:04,160
when economists were talking about deregulating airlines.

400
01:56:04,160 --> 01:56:06,720
And there was the idea of, look, even if there's only one

401
01:56:06,720 --> 01:56:10,000
airline servicing a certain regional airport,

402
01:56:10,000 --> 01:56:11,840
there's a lot of airlines in the world.

403
01:56:11,840 --> 01:56:15,080
So if the one airline that's there starts raising its prices,

404
01:56:15,080 --> 01:56:16,360
this will go into track entry.

405
01:56:16,360 --> 01:56:20,320
And that will prevent them from doing so.

406
01:56:20,320 --> 01:56:24,000
So subsequently, we pointed out this is probably

407
01:56:24,000 --> 01:56:29,280
exaggerated, although the basic point of if an airline is

408
01:56:29,280 --> 01:56:30,960
permanently really profitable in an area,

409
01:56:30,960 --> 01:56:33,200
then they're likely to go and get some other firm that

410
01:56:33,200 --> 01:56:33,960
wants to go there.

411
01:56:33,960 --> 01:56:35,440
But it may not happen that quickly.

412
01:56:35,440 --> 01:56:37,400
People put a lot of points about how planes can

413
01:56:37,400 --> 01:56:39,080
move around really easily.

414
01:56:39,080 --> 01:56:41,360
So yeah, you can move the planes around really easily.

415
01:56:41,360 --> 01:56:42,840
But you're working on the contracts

416
01:56:42,840 --> 01:56:46,800
to get access to the terminal and things like that.

417
01:56:46,800 --> 01:56:49,000
That's probably not going to happen.

418
01:56:49,000 --> 01:56:53,320
Anyway, so the basic set of contestability models is this.

419
01:56:53,320 --> 01:56:57,160
First, you've got an incumbent firm that sets its price.

420
01:56:57,160 --> 01:56:58,640
So normally, you call it P sub i.

421
01:56:58,640 --> 01:57:01,200
So that's the price of the incumbent.

422
01:57:01,200 --> 01:57:03,440
And then a potential entrant looks at the mark

423
01:57:03,440 --> 01:57:06,560
and decides whether he wants to enter.

424
01:57:06,560 --> 01:57:09,200
And if he enters, then the entrant sets the price.

425
01:57:09,200 --> 01:57:10,480
And then customers are scammed.

426
01:57:10,480 --> 01:57:13,240
And if he does enter, then his price is called PE.

427
01:57:13,240 --> 01:57:15,400
And then consumers just buy from whoever

428
01:57:15,400 --> 01:57:17,360
sells at the cheaper price.

429
01:57:17,360 --> 01:57:18,760
Simple point.

430
01:57:18,760 --> 01:57:19,760
OK.

431
01:57:19,760 --> 01:57:21,360
All right, so what's going to happen?

432
01:57:21,360 --> 01:57:23,280
Well, suppose that we got total cost is just

433
01:57:23,280 --> 01:57:27,920
equal to B times quantity.

434
01:57:27,920 --> 01:57:29,440
And then we can set this up.

435
01:57:29,440 --> 01:57:32,600
So if total cost is just B times quantity,

436
01:57:32,600 --> 01:57:35,000
then marginal cost looks like what?

437
01:57:35,000 --> 01:57:39,400
What is marginal cost if total cost equals BQ?

438
01:57:39,400 --> 01:57:42,960
The partial derivative of cost with respect to Q, which is?

439
01:57:42,960 --> 01:57:43,480
B.

440
01:57:43,480 --> 01:57:43,980
B.

441
01:57:43,980 --> 01:57:46,440
So not that.

442
01:57:46,440 --> 01:57:47,560
That total cost.

443
01:57:47,560 --> 01:57:48,400
Yes, yes, yes.

444
01:57:48,400 --> 01:57:50,040
Yeah, total cost is that.

445
01:57:50,040 --> 01:57:51,480
But the derivative is that.

446
01:57:51,480 --> 01:57:52,480
Yes.

447
01:57:52,480 --> 01:57:55,120
Yes, so B. So that's your partial cost.

448
01:57:55,120 --> 01:57:58,840
Partial cost equals B. All right.

449
01:57:58,840 --> 01:58:00,880
OK, so what's going to happen?

450
01:58:00,880 --> 01:58:04,520
Well, so suppose that the incumbent sets a price that

451
01:58:04,520 --> 01:58:07,040
is greater than B. All right.

452
01:58:07,040 --> 01:58:10,400
So if the incumbent sets a price greater than B,

453
01:58:10,400 --> 01:58:12,680
then the entrant will come and take a look.

454
01:58:12,680 --> 01:58:15,400
And he will see, here's the man curve here.

455
01:58:15,400 --> 01:58:17,800
All right, so if the incumbent goes and sets a price here

456
01:58:17,800 --> 01:58:19,920
greater than B, so that's PI.

457
01:58:19,920 --> 01:58:22,920
Now, if you're the entrant, you have a really easy strategy

458
01:58:22,920 --> 01:58:25,920
that you can apply, which will make you money for sure.

459
01:58:25,920 --> 01:58:28,840
And well, so he's charging PI.

460
01:58:28,840 --> 01:58:30,120
What do you want to charge?

461
01:58:30,120 --> 01:58:30,620
P.

462
01:58:30,620 --> 01:58:31,120
Yes, I know.

463
01:58:31,120 --> 01:58:32,040
I have PI.

464
01:58:32,040 --> 01:58:37,080
So PE equals PI minus an epsilon, minus a penny.

465
01:58:37,080 --> 01:58:39,640
Charge the price one penny below the incumbent.

466
01:58:39,640 --> 01:58:42,960
And then the result is that you steal the entire market

467
01:58:42,960 --> 01:58:44,680
from him.

468
01:58:44,680 --> 01:58:48,000
And the incumbent gets no money.

469
01:58:48,000 --> 01:58:49,640
All right, so if the incumbent realizes

470
01:58:49,640 --> 01:58:51,160
this is what's going to happen, then he

471
01:58:51,160 --> 01:58:53,240
must find out and charge a lower price than that.

472
01:58:53,240 --> 01:58:54,960
But then the entrant can go and charge

473
01:58:54,960 --> 01:58:57,200
a price an epsilon below that.

474
01:58:57,200 --> 01:59:00,600
So if you think it through, then you say, all right, well,

475
01:59:00,600 --> 01:59:01,160
so let's see.

476
01:59:01,160 --> 01:59:03,400
It's like, nope, the incumbent can never charge a price

477
01:59:03,400 --> 01:59:04,440
over marginal cost.

478
01:59:04,440 --> 01:59:07,000
Would the incumbent ever charge a price

479
01:59:07,000 --> 01:59:08,600
that's less than marginal cost?

480
01:59:08,600 --> 01:59:09,480
That's idiotic, right?

481
01:59:09,480 --> 01:59:11,100
You're just losing money on everything.

482
01:59:11,100 --> 01:59:13,680
Yes, you do get to run the market, but you lose money.

483
01:59:13,680 --> 01:59:15,880
So if he's never going to charge a price over marginal cost,

484
01:59:15,880 --> 01:59:17,760
never going to charge a price under marginal cost,

485
01:59:17,760 --> 01:59:19,640
how about a charge of price exactly equal to marginal cost?

486
01:59:19,640 --> 01:59:21,440
Is that a possible equilibrium?

487
01:59:21,440 --> 01:59:23,080
And the answer is it is.

488
01:59:23,080 --> 01:59:25,840
So the incumbent charges a price equal to marginal cost.

489
01:59:25,840 --> 01:59:28,400
And then this does not provoke any entry.

490
01:59:28,400 --> 01:59:30,600
And so the incumbent is a monopolist,

491
01:59:30,600 --> 01:59:33,680
but he's a monopolist who acts exactly like a perfect event

492
01:59:33,680 --> 01:59:35,160
here.

493
01:59:35,160 --> 01:59:38,160
And that is the amazing result of the simple contestability

494
01:59:38,160 --> 01:59:39,520
model.

495
01:59:39,520 --> 01:59:42,280
In fact, the only Nash equilibrium

496
01:59:42,280 --> 01:59:45,520
is one where the incumbent charges a price equal to b,

497
01:59:45,520 --> 01:59:47,200
and then there's no entry.

498
01:59:47,200 --> 01:59:48,120
So there it is.

499
01:59:50,960 --> 01:59:52,320
Now, what's so amazing about this?

500
01:59:52,320 --> 01:59:55,400
Well, did anyone ever have, say, an intermediate microeconomics

501
01:59:55,400 --> 01:59:57,680
textbook that says that markets fail

502
01:59:57,680 --> 01:59:59,960
unless there's perfect combination?

503
01:59:59,960 --> 01:60:00,520
Did anyone have one?


1
01:60:00,000 --> 01:60:03,080
There are many intermediate micro-techs that say this.

2
01:60:03,080 --> 01:60:04,520
One side is the referee, for one.

3
01:60:04,520 --> 01:60:07,240
And I went and just pointed out, look,

4
01:60:07,240 --> 01:60:09,480
there's a list of all the statements in the book that

5
01:60:09,480 --> 01:60:11,440
are incorrect.

6
01:60:11,440 --> 01:60:13,720
So anyway, it is absolutely incorrect

7
01:60:13,720 --> 01:60:18,000
to say, as a matter of economic theory, that markets fail,

8
01:60:18,000 --> 01:60:21,200
there is a perfect competition.

9
01:60:21,200 --> 01:60:23,960
Say, how about a contestable monopoly?

10
01:60:23,960 --> 01:60:25,640
What does a contestable monopoly do?

11
01:60:25,640 --> 01:60:27,760
It does exactly what a perfectly competitive industry

12
01:60:27,760 --> 01:60:30,040
would have done.

13
01:60:30,040 --> 01:60:31,600
Now you can say that's not realistic.

14
01:60:31,600 --> 01:60:32,840
Then you say, yeah, well, perfect competition

15
01:60:32,840 --> 01:60:33,840
isn't realistic either.

16
01:60:33,840 --> 01:60:36,720
So it's, you know, it's, but if you're

17
01:60:36,720 --> 01:60:38,800
saying that unless you have perfect competition,

18
01:60:38,800 --> 01:60:40,240
then the market fails, no.

19
01:60:40,240 --> 01:60:43,160
Here's another model, which is actually

20
01:60:43,160 --> 01:60:44,960
about as different from perfect competition

21
01:60:44,960 --> 01:60:46,920
as you can imagine, structurally.

22
01:60:46,920 --> 01:60:48,340
It's like perfect competition, you

23
01:60:48,340 --> 01:60:49,720
have an infinite number of firms.

24
01:60:49,720 --> 01:60:52,120
Here we're down to one firm, but the one firm

25
01:60:52,120 --> 01:60:57,360
acts like there's an infinite number of firms, which is,

26
01:60:57,360 --> 01:60:59,000
I would say, a surprising result.

27
01:60:59,000 --> 01:61:02,080
So it's possible, it is, but it's

28
01:61:02,080 --> 01:61:04,360
possible that one firm could be just as

29
01:61:04,360 --> 01:61:05,760
good as perfect competition.

30
01:61:05,760 --> 01:61:09,720
Monopoly could work just like perfect competition.

31
01:61:09,720 --> 01:61:12,280
Any of you ever thought about Amazon?

32
01:61:12,280 --> 01:61:14,320
Like, is this world with Amazon, is this better or worse

33
01:61:14,320 --> 01:61:16,560
than a world with like 1,000 different online sellers?

34
01:61:19,480 --> 01:61:21,000
I don't know, I kind of like Amazon.

35
01:61:21,000 --> 01:61:23,680
OK.

36
01:61:23,680 --> 01:61:25,800
I never have to think about, who are these other firms,

37
01:61:25,800 --> 01:61:27,840
and what's their deal, and what are they selling?

38
01:61:27,840 --> 01:61:29,080
I have no idea what they're selling, like,

39
01:61:29,080 --> 01:61:31,880
because Amazon, that's a great deal every time, practically.

40
01:61:31,880 --> 01:61:34,600
Would there be an HMO for online sellers?

41
01:61:34,600 --> 01:61:37,160
Yeah, there could be, yeah, that's possible.

42
01:61:37,160 --> 01:61:38,760
Could be that.

43
01:61:38,760 --> 01:61:42,160
Of course, then, do I have to go shop for 1,000 HMOs?

44
01:61:42,160 --> 01:61:44,840
Or it would not be an HMO, it would be like a CMO,

45
01:61:44,840 --> 01:61:46,600
a customer maintenance organization,

46
01:61:46,600 --> 01:61:50,040
consumer maintenance organization.

47
01:61:50,040 --> 01:61:52,800
All right, now, to be fair, it only

48
01:61:52,800 --> 01:61:57,440
works exactly this way if the entering and the incumbent

49
01:61:57,440 --> 01:61:59,520
have exactly the same cost curves.

50
01:61:59,520 --> 01:62:01,520
So what if the entering actually has higher cost

51
01:62:01,520 --> 01:62:02,360
than the incumbent?

52
01:62:04,680 --> 01:62:06,320
Which is a pretty reasonable assumption,

53
01:62:06,320 --> 01:62:09,000
because it explains why is the incumbent the incumbent.

54
01:62:09,000 --> 01:62:10,160
No reason.

55
01:62:10,160 --> 01:62:11,520
It doesn't sound very satisfying.

56
01:62:11,520 --> 01:62:13,440
How about, why is the incumbent the incumbent?

57
01:62:13,440 --> 01:62:15,120
Because he's better than everybody else.

58
01:62:15,120 --> 01:62:17,040
That sounds like a pretty good reason.

59
01:62:17,040 --> 01:62:20,200
Like, in a sporting event, if they're like the Unreturned

60
01:62:20,200 --> 01:62:24,200
Champion, Muhammad Ali, if that's your story,

61
01:62:24,200 --> 01:62:28,280
well, why do you think Muhammad Ali is the returning champion?

62
01:62:28,280 --> 01:62:30,480
Well, there's no reason, just luck.

63
01:62:30,480 --> 01:62:31,280
There's that story.

64
01:62:31,280 --> 01:62:34,240
Or how about because he's the best?

65
01:62:34,240 --> 01:62:35,720
So the greatest of all time?

66
01:62:35,720 --> 01:62:38,040
Greatest, yeah, yeah, the greatest.

67
01:62:38,040 --> 01:62:40,040
So that is another story.

68
01:62:40,040 --> 01:62:41,920
Gee, you might be exaggerated, or of course,

69
01:62:41,920 --> 01:62:43,680
you might not remain the greatest forever.

70
01:62:43,680 --> 01:62:45,400
But still, a pretty good starting place

71
01:62:45,400 --> 01:62:46,820
is, yeah, well, incumbents probably

72
01:62:46,820 --> 01:62:49,240
are there for a good reason, namely, they're better.

73
01:62:49,240 --> 01:62:53,960
So what happens then if we've got a marginal cost

74
01:62:53,960 --> 01:62:56,600
curve here for the incumbent and a marginal cost

75
01:62:56,600 --> 01:63:01,240
curve here for the entrant, and then here is demand?

76
01:63:01,240 --> 01:63:04,720
And again, now here you might say, well,

77
01:63:04,720 --> 01:63:06,180
maybe the incumbent will still just

78
01:63:06,180 --> 01:63:09,280
have to charge a price equal to his own marginal cost.

79
01:63:09,280 --> 01:63:10,320
But that's wrong.

80
01:63:10,320 --> 01:63:11,360
Because here's the thing.

81
01:63:11,360 --> 01:63:13,120
Suppose you're the incumbent, you're charging this price,

82
01:63:13,120 --> 01:63:14,120
and then you get greedy.

83
01:63:14,120 --> 01:63:17,760
You say, hey, let's raise the price by a unit.

84
01:63:17,760 --> 01:63:19,760
Now, when you raise the price by a penny,

85
01:63:19,760 --> 01:63:21,480
does that provoke entry?

86
01:63:21,480 --> 01:63:22,360
No.

87
01:63:22,360 --> 01:63:25,440
No, because you're still below the entrant's costs.

88
01:63:25,440 --> 01:63:27,280
So even though you're making a profit,

89
01:63:27,280 --> 01:63:30,120
he would be making a loss if he entered against you

90
01:63:30,120 --> 01:63:32,160
at a price low enough to win your customers over,

91
01:63:32,160 --> 01:63:34,000
so he doesn't want to do that.

92
01:63:34,000 --> 01:63:35,880
And you could raise it more, raise it more,

93
01:63:35,880 --> 01:63:37,720
raise it more, raise it more.

94
01:63:37,720 --> 01:63:40,280
Each of these places, as you're raising the price,

95
01:63:40,280 --> 01:63:44,480
you are, again, assuming that demand elasticity doesn't

96
01:63:44,480 --> 01:63:47,600
kick in and mess things up for you,

97
01:63:47,600 --> 01:63:52,880
you are actually getting more and more profit out of it.

98
01:63:52,880 --> 01:63:58,040
And then finally, you get to within an epsilon of his costs.

99
01:63:58,040 --> 01:63:59,440
And that is where you need to be.

100
01:63:59,440 --> 01:64:00,940
And that is where you need to worry.

101
01:64:00,940 --> 01:64:02,960
Because if you actually got to his costs,

102
01:64:02,960 --> 01:64:05,880
then the entrant could go and enter,

103
01:64:05,880 --> 01:64:08,320
and he would share the market with you,

104
01:64:08,320 --> 01:64:10,400
and would be no worse off than you,

105
01:64:10,400 --> 01:64:12,640
and would at least break even.

106
01:64:12,640 --> 01:64:14,200
So again, the standard result here

107
01:64:14,200 --> 01:64:18,040
is that if the incumbent has lower costs,

108
01:64:18,040 --> 01:64:20,640
then the incumbent is going to set a price

109
01:64:20,640 --> 01:64:23,440
equal to the marginal cost of the entrant, again,

110
01:64:23,440 --> 01:64:24,960
minus an epsilon.

111
01:64:24,960 --> 01:64:25,920
It's minus an epsilon.

112
01:64:25,920 --> 01:64:27,000
It's minus, say, a penny.

113
01:64:30,000 --> 01:64:33,920
So the incumbent price is just below the entrant's costs.

114
01:64:33,920 --> 01:64:36,440
And then you will look at the end of the incumbent,

115
01:64:36,440 --> 01:64:39,120
and is the incumbent making a profit?

116
01:64:39,120 --> 01:64:42,200
Do you want to make a profit in equilibrium?

117
01:64:42,200 --> 01:64:43,520
Absolutely.

118
01:64:43,520 --> 01:64:45,140
Is there any deadweight loss from what

119
01:64:45,140 --> 01:64:46,760
the incumbent is doing?

120
01:64:46,760 --> 01:64:47,520
Tiniest.

121
01:64:47,520 --> 01:64:49,160
Yeah, yeah, there is.

122
01:64:49,160 --> 01:64:51,920
So actually, it's just the usual thing here,

123
01:64:51,920 --> 01:64:56,520
where his profit will be this rectangle here.

124
01:64:56,520 --> 01:64:59,320
And there'll be a deadweight loss triangle there.

125
01:64:59,320 --> 01:65:01,240
Now, in the real world, you're probably

126
01:65:01,240 --> 01:65:02,640
right that that triangle is small,

127
01:65:02,640 --> 01:65:07,000
although the size depends upon the gap between the best

128
01:65:07,000 --> 01:65:10,320
firm in the world and the second best firm in the world.

129
01:65:10,320 --> 01:65:14,760
So in the same way that if you want to win a race,

130
01:65:14,760 --> 01:65:16,040
how do you win a race?

131
01:65:16,040 --> 01:65:19,440
By being faster than the second fastest racer.

132
01:65:19,440 --> 01:65:20,640
And it's the same idea here.

133
01:65:20,640 --> 01:65:21,960
How do you get the market?

134
01:65:21,960 --> 01:65:24,680
By going and pricing just below the cost

135
01:65:24,680 --> 01:65:27,600
of the second best competitor in the world,

136
01:65:27,600 --> 01:65:29,440
second most efficient producer in the world.

137
01:65:32,600 --> 01:65:35,560
And anyway, of course, again, like in the case of racing,

138
01:65:35,560 --> 01:65:38,480
you probably know how small the gap between the first

139
01:65:38,480 --> 01:65:40,680
and the second fastest person in the world is.

140
01:65:40,680 --> 01:65:43,520
Sometimes measured in like hundredths of a second.

141
01:65:43,520 --> 01:65:46,880
So in business, it may not be quite that close.

142
01:65:46,880 --> 01:65:49,840
But still, how big is the gap?

143
01:65:49,840 --> 01:65:51,480
Or can the gap reasonably be?

144
01:65:51,480 --> 01:65:52,840
That is crucial.

145
01:65:52,840 --> 01:65:54,680
Again, do you have a question over there?

146
01:65:54,680 --> 01:65:57,080
Yes.

147
01:65:57,080 --> 01:65:59,280
So if the incubant stand the price

148
01:65:59,280 --> 01:66:06,480
at the cost of the entrant, then there is a weak dominance.

149
01:66:06,480 --> 01:66:07,000
Yeah, yeah.

150
01:66:07,000 --> 01:66:09,960
There is a weak dominance.

151
01:66:09,960 --> 01:66:10,480
Yeah, yeah.

152
01:66:10,480 --> 01:66:12,480
Yes, so there is weak dominance.

153
01:66:12,480 --> 01:66:16,080
So there is an equilibrium where the entrant still doesn't

154
01:66:16,080 --> 01:66:16,760
produce.

155
01:66:16,760 --> 01:66:18,960
And then you make an infinitesimal amount

156
01:66:18,960 --> 01:66:19,800
of extra money.

157
01:66:19,800 --> 01:66:21,120
Although, of course, there's also

158
01:66:21,120 --> 01:66:23,240
an equilibrium where the entrant enters.

159
01:66:23,240 --> 01:66:25,480
And then you lose half your profits,

160
01:66:25,480 --> 01:66:30,440
which is really bad, right?

161
01:66:30,440 --> 01:66:30,940
Yes.

162
01:66:30,940 --> 01:66:32,760
In that case, you probably want to stay

163
01:66:32,760 --> 01:66:34,600
under a wider margin than that.

164
01:66:34,600 --> 01:66:35,480
Yeah, yeah.

165
01:66:35,480 --> 01:66:37,960
So you're actually like, so in the real world,

166
01:66:37,960 --> 01:66:40,160
where things might bounce around all the time,

167
01:66:40,160 --> 01:66:43,840
or you might make a mistake about the entrance costs,

168
01:66:43,840 --> 01:66:45,720
then you want to have a bigger buffer.

169
01:66:45,720 --> 01:66:49,200
Just like if you are a running champion,

170
01:66:49,200 --> 01:66:51,640
if you know for sure how good the second best person is,

171
01:66:51,640 --> 01:66:53,960
you can just train to be a little bit faster than him.

172
01:66:53,960 --> 01:66:55,880
But if there's some uncertainty, then you

173
01:66:55,880 --> 01:66:57,240
really want to be the champion.

174
01:66:57,240 --> 01:66:59,040
Then you need to do even more than that

175
01:66:59,040 --> 01:67:00,520
to give yourself a margin of error.

176
01:67:00,520 --> 01:67:03,000
Right.

177
01:67:03,000 --> 01:67:04,640
Makes a lot of sense.

178
01:67:04,640 --> 01:67:06,560
All right, now what if there are fixed costs?

179
01:67:06,560 --> 01:67:08,200
So what if we have a cost function that

180
01:67:08,200 --> 01:67:12,800
looks like total cost is equal to A plus B times Q?

181
01:67:12,800 --> 01:67:16,560
And you guys all remember what that cost function looks like?

182
01:67:16,560 --> 01:67:19,040
So we're like, particularly like the associated average cost

183
01:67:19,040 --> 01:67:20,080
function.

184
01:67:20,080 --> 01:67:21,560
So total cost equals that.

185
01:67:21,560 --> 01:67:27,320
Then average cost is going to be equal to A over Q plus B.

186
01:67:27,320 --> 01:67:30,960
So basically, this means that a quantity of 0 is infinite.

187
01:67:30,960 --> 01:67:34,160
Then it asymptotes down to B. So remember this.

188
01:67:34,160 --> 01:67:36,240
That's what your average cost looks like.

189
01:67:36,240 --> 01:67:40,720
All right, so now in here, we go and we put a demand curve here.

190
01:67:40,720 --> 01:67:42,560
Now again, we can go back to the assumption

191
01:67:42,560 --> 01:67:45,600
that both firms have identical costs, actually.

192
01:67:45,600 --> 01:67:48,320
So we'll assume both firms have identical costs.

193
01:67:48,320 --> 01:67:51,400
Right now, notice marginal cost is still going to be B.

194
01:67:51,400 --> 01:67:52,840
So to differentiate that function,

195
01:67:52,840 --> 01:67:56,720
the marginal cost will just be, which is not coincidentally,

196
01:67:56,720 --> 01:67:59,360
the asymptote to which the average cost is going.

197
01:67:59,360 --> 01:68:02,600
So that's going to be marginal cost.

198
01:68:02,600 --> 01:68:04,400
So marginal cost over there.

199
01:68:04,400 --> 01:68:06,480
All right, now here's the question.

200
01:68:06,480 --> 01:68:09,680
If you suppose, so what will happen

201
01:68:09,680 --> 01:68:14,640
if the incumbent goes and sets a price equal to marginal cost?

202
01:68:14,640 --> 01:68:16,440
Well, let's see.

203
01:68:16,440 --> 01:68:18,880
If you go and set a price equal to marginal cost, let's see.

204
01:68:18,880 --> 01:68:24,560
So we've got PQ minus total cost equals profit.

205
01:68:24,560 --> 01:68:26,800
And if you set your price equal to marginal cost,

206
01:68:26,800 --> 01:68:30,320
then that would mean you're going to have your revenue will

207
01:68:30,320 --> 01:68:36,400
be B times Q, and your cost will be A plus BQ.

208
01:68:36,400 --> 01:68:39,200
And you may notice the two terms cancel.

209
01:68:39,200 --> 01:68:40,360
BQs cancel.

210
01:68:40,360 --> 01:68:44,320
And what does that leave the incumbent with?

211
01:68:44,320 --> 01:68:46,040
Negative B profit.

212
01:68:46,040 --> 01:68:49,080
So it means that you are not able to cover your fixed costs

213
01:68:49,080 --> 01:68:53,240
at all, and then you are losing money.

214
01:68:53,240 --> 01:68:57,400
So there is no longer going to have an equilibrium where

215
01:68:57,400 --> 01:68:59,320
you charge a price when the incumbent charges

216
01:68:59,320 --> 01:69:02,840
equal to marginal cost.

217
01:69:02,840 --> 01:69:05,000
So if you were this incumbent, the natural thing to do

218
01:69:05,000 --> 01:69:07,480
is, well, why don't I go and raise my price a little bit?

219
01:69:07,480 --> 01:69:10,080
And if you do raise your price a little bit, what you find

220
01:69:10,080 --> 01:69:13,440
is the incumbent still, the entrant still

221
01:69:13,440 --> 01:69:17,520
stays out because he'll still lose money if he enters.

222
01:69:17,520 --> 01:69:19,880
And you still lose money because you're still

223
01:69:19,880 --> 01:69:21,400
below average cost.

224
01:69:21,400 --> 01:69:22,640
But here's the good thing.

225
01:69:22,640 --> 01:69:26,360
You're losing less money because the quantity is lower

226
01:69:26,360 --> 01:69:27,720
and the price is higher.

227
01:69:27,720 --> 01:69:32,800
And so you're going to be losing less money as a result.

228
01:69:32,800 --> 01:69:36,600
So then you want to keep raising price until you get to here.

229
01:69:36,600 --> 01:69:38,840
Price equals average cost.

230
01:69:38,840 --> 01:69:40,720
And at that point, suppose that you

231
01:69:40,720 --> 01:69:42,640
were to raise your price by a little bit more.

232
01:69:42,640 --> 01:69:45,320
Then what would happen?

233
01:69:45,320 --> 01:69:47,320
So if the incumbent has a steady price

234
01:69:47,320 --> 01:69:49,840
equal to average cost, you raise it a little bit more

235
01:69:49,840 --> 01:69:51,040
so you're there.

236
01:69:51,040 --> 01:69:52,680
Then what would the entrant want to do?

237
01:69:52,680 --> 01:69:53,360
Come in.

238
01:69:53,360 --> 01:69:54,720
Then the entrant says, yeah, I'll

239
01:69:54,720 --> 01:69:57,840
come in and go in between average cost

240
01:69:57,840 --> 01:70:00,160
and I'll go there.

241
01:70:00,160 --> 01:70:02,080
And that's the whole market for him.

242
01:70:02,080 --> 01:70:04,240
So then this gives us a result that if we

243
01:70:04,240 --> 01:70:08,000
have a contestable monopoly and there are fixed costs,

244
01:70:08,000 --> 01:70:11,800
then the result is the market goes to a price of at a set

245
01:70:11,800 --> 01:70:13,880
price equal to average cost.

246
01:70:13,880 --> 01:70:15,880
Is there any deadweight cost when you set price

247
01:70:15,880 --> 01:70:18,440
equal to average cost?

248
01:70:18,440 --> 01:70:21,320
Remember, we talked about this in week one.

249
01:70:21,320 --> 01:70:22,280
Yeah, there is.

250
01:70:22,280 --> 01:70:25,000
As I draw that, you have to take out your microscope to see it.

251
01:70:25,000 --> 01:70:26,360
Because let's see.

252
01:70:26,360 --> 01:70:27,080
So here we are.

253
01:70:27,080 --> 01:70:30,960
So basically, that will be your deadweight loss.

254
01:70:30,960 --> 01:70:32,560
So those are all the units where you're

255
01:70:32,560 --> 01:70:35,000
charging above marginal cost.

256
01:70:35,000 --> 01:70:37,240
And as a result, they are not sold.

257
01:70:37,240 --> 01:70:40,840
Because people only want to pay that marginal cost.

258
01:70:40,840 --> 01:70:43,080
Of course, this is all assuming that you have to have

259
01:70:43,080 --> 01:70:44,080
uniform pricing, right?

260
01:70:46,920 --> 01:70:48,520
All right, now one other thing to note.

261
01:70:48,520 --> 01:70:51,080
So when we say the equilibrium pricing with average cost,

262
01:70:51,080 --> 01:70:53,760
you notice that here, actually, average cost or the demand

263
01:70:53,760 --> 01:70:55,920
curve crosses average cost twice.

264
01:70:55,920 --> 01:70:58,280
So does this mean we have two equilibria?

265
01:70:58,280 --> 01:71:00,200
And what do you think about that equilibrium?

266
01:71:00,200 --> 01:71:02,200
Is that an OK equilibrium?

267
01:71:02,200 --> 01:71:03,400
Or that possible equilibrium?

268
01:71:03,400 --> 01:71:06,480
Is this one OK?

269
01:71:06,480 --> 01:71:08,800
Well, suppose the incumbent is pricing there.

270
01:71:08,800 --> 01:71:10,560
What might you as a vendor want to do?

271
01:71:10,560 --> 01:71:11,840
Price them for lower.

272
01:71:11,840 --> 01:71:14,880
Yeah, or how about just price one penny less?

273
01:71:14,880 --> 01:71:15,600
Price one less.

274
01:71:15,600 --> 01:71:17,440
And notice, this is demand.

275
01:71:17,440 --> 01:71:18,280
That's average cost.

276
01:71:18,280 --> 01:71:21,880
So you are making a profit on every single unit.

277
01:71:21,880 --> 01:71:24,960
So even though there likely is possible for the demand

278
01:71:24,960 --> 01:71:27,720
curve to cross the average cost curve in multiple places,

279
01:71:27,720 --> 01:71:29,400
the equilibrium in the testability model

280
01:71:29,400 --> 01:71:34,200
is given by the lowest price of all the intersections.

281
01:71:34,200 --> 01:71:38,840
Which again is a nice qualifying exam question.

282
01:71:38,840 --> 01:71:41,880
So here is demand.

283
01:71:41,880 --> 01:71:43,840
Here's the average cost curve.

284
01:71:43,840 --> 01:71:45,200
Multiple crossing points.

285
01:71:45,200 --> 01:71:46,800
How many of the equilibrium are there?

286
01:71:46,800 --> 01:71:48,440
The answer is one.

287
01:71:48,440 --> 01:71:50,320
One.

288
01:71:50,320 --> 01:71:53,320
Take all the intersections and then find the lowest price,

289
01:71:53,320 --> 01:71:55,640
the one that's most favorable to consumers.

290
01:71:55,640 --> 01:71:59,080
Because in all this range here, actually, these

291
01:71:59,080 --> 01:72:01,240
are all profitable from entrance.

292
01:72:01,240 --> 01:72:02,640
And in fact, if you look closely,

293
01:72:02,640 --> 01:72:05,640
you'll see that just as I branch geometrically,

294
01:72:05,640 --> 01:72:07,640
this is actually probably a lot less profitable

295
01:72:07,640 --> 01:72:08,560
than say that or that.

296
01:72:08,560 --> 01:72:12,200
Because there we have more units and we have a bigger profit

297
01:72:12,200 --> 01:72:13,280
per unit.

298
01:72:13,280 --> 01:72:16,440
So if you increase quantity and per unit profits,

299
01:72:16,440 --> 01:72:18,120
you will automatically increase profit.

300
01:72:23,240 --> 01:72:25,800
So how do you feel about this?

301
01:72:25,800 --> 01:72:27,640
Happy?

302
01:72:27,640 --> 01:72:28,840
Energetic?

303
01:72:28,840 --> 01:72:33,640
Feeling as good as you can at 9.43 in the evening

304
01:72:33,640 --> 01:72:36,280
about the testability model?

305
01:72:36,280 --> 01:72:40,640
Feeling as much affection as you can for any economic model?

306
01:72:40,640 --> 01:72:42,360
I will say, so this is a model that

307
01:72:42,360 --> 01:72:47,280
has gotten a lot of flack, and yet not only does it,

308
01:72:47,280 --> 01:72:50,680
I think it explains a lot about Amazon or Netflix

309
01:72:50,680 --> 01:72:53,640
or so many of the other amazing new companies that

310
01:72:53,640 --> 01:72:56,240
have come into being in the last couple of decades,

311
01:72:56,240 --> 01:72:58,920
but furthermore, it explains much more mundane things.

312
01:72:58,920 --> 01:73:01,160
Like imagine that you get a letter home tonight saying

313
01:73:01,160 --> 01:73:04,360
that a distant uncle has died in Hayes, Kansas,

314
01:73:04,360 --> 01:73:06,800
and you are now the proud owner of the only grocery store

315
01:73:06,800 --> 01:73:08,320
in Hayes, Kansas.

316
01:73:08,320 --> 01:73:10,720
How many people look at this letter and go,

317
01:73:10,720 --> 01:73:13,320
and I will now starve them into submission.

318
01:73:13,320 --> 01:73:18,160
They will pay any price that I want or die.

319
01:73:18,160 --> 01:73:20,120
Is that a reasonable reaction to finding out

320
01:73:20,120 --> 01:73:25,800
that you own the only grocery store in a small town?

321
01:73:25,800 --> 01:73:28,440
I have no proof that that is not a reasonable reaction,

322
01:73:28,440 --> 01:73:32,720
but that seems like a ridiculous reaction to almost everyone.

323
01:73:32,720 --> 01:73:34,720
And again, testability explains why.

324
01:73:34,720 --> 01:73:37,920
So look, sure, you could go and jack up your prices a lot

325
01:73:37,920 --> 01:73:40,000
the first day you take over management,

326
01:73:40,000 --> 01:73:42,880
but how long is it going to be before someone else comes up

327
01:73:42,880 --> 01:73:46,800
and says, you know, Joe's uncle is a terrible guy,

328
01:73:46,800 --> 01:73:49,280
and we all hate him, and we hate what he's done to the store,

329
01:73:49,280 --> 01:73:52,000
and now I'm going to have a store where things are

330
01:73:52,000 --> 01:73:53,280
the way they're supposed to be.

331
01:73:53,280 --> 01:73:55,960
Ack, what a good old Joe is running a grocery store.

332
01:73:55,960 --> 01:73:57,760
And too bad Joe picked such a bad heir,

333
01:73:57,760 --> 01:74:00,000
because his store is going to disappear.

334
01:74:00,000 --> 01:74:02,960
No one wants to shop there anymore.

335
01:74:02,960 --> 01:74:04,920
And if you just look at how many monopolies

336
01:74:04,920 --> 01:74:09,600
there are in the real world, if you wanted to go and spend,

337
01:74:09,600 --> 01:74:11,800
imagine that you are the car.

338
01:74:11,800 --> 01:74:16,720
So you are on campus, and you want to get a tailor.

339
01:74:16,720 --> 01:74:19,600
So you get a tailor within walking distance.

340
01:74:19,600 --> 01:74:22,480
So I recommend the one at the University Mall.

341
01:74:22,480 --> 01:74:28,080
So very high quality work, a no, a positive attitude,

342
01:74:28,080 --> 01:74:30,080
can you have it done today?

343
01:74:30,080 --> 01:74:31,800
All right, great.

344
01:74:31,800 --> 01:74:35,600
So anyway, is this Taylor monopoly?

345
01:74:35,600 --> 01:74:39,640
In some sense, but does it act like a monopoly

346
01:74:39,640 --> 01:74:43,520
that you would see in a Steven Seagal movie or something

347
01:74:43,520 --> 01:74:44,640
like this?

348
01:74:44,640 --> 01:74:46,560
Right, no, it doesn't seem like it does.

349
01:74:46,560 --> 01:74:49,920
Because they know, if we don't make people happy,

350
01:74:49,920 --> 01:74:53,320
then there are other places that could provide the service,

351
01:74:53,320 --> 01:74:55,880
even though they are invisible.

352
01:74:55,880 --> 01:74:59,360
So this is another great example of the invisible hand.

353
01:74:59,360 --> 01:75:01,840
These are the firms that don't exist that still

354
01:75:01,840 --> 01:75:03,840
have great power in the world.

355
01:75:03,840 --> 01:75:08,240
These are the non-existent, yet greatly influential firms.

356
01:75:08,240 --> 01:75:10,760
If that sounds mystical, it's because you have not

357
01:75:10,760 --> 01:75:11,760
understood the argument.

358
01:75:11,760 --> 01:75:13,120
Once you understand the argument,

359
01:75:13,120 --> 01:75:16,400
of course non-existent things can control our destinies.

360
01:75:16,400 --> 01:75:17,400
It makes perfect sense.

361
01:75:20,600 --> 01:75:24,840
Because non-existent things do not mean never-existent things.

362
01:75:24,840 --> 01:75:27,120
They are things that could exist if conditions change,

363
01:75:27,120 --> 01:75:30,280
which can totally change behavior.

364
01:75:30,280 --> 01:75:32,120
All right, that is fixed costs.

365
01:75:32,120 --> 01:75:37,680
Then we get to another fun case, the case of the sunk cost.

366
01:75:37,680 --> 01:75:40,680
What is a sunk cost?

367
01:75:40,680 --> 01:75:43,200
Suppose there's a sunk cost of A followed by price decisions.

368
01:75:43,200 --> 01:75:44,560
So remember, what's the difference

369
01:75:44,560 --> 01:75:47,040
between a fixed cost and a sunk cost?

370
01:75:47,040 --> 01:75:48,560
Sunk cost can't be found.

371
01:75:48,560 --> 01:75:51,080
Yes, yes, you could recover your fixed cost.

372
01:75:51,080 --> 01:75:53,640
So you could go and resell the factory and get your A back

373
01:75:53,640 --> 01:75:55,920
if you decide you don't want the factory.

374
01:75:55,920 --> 01:75:57,360
Sunk cost, on the other hand, you

375
01:75:57,360 --> 01:76:01,640
have to burn the A to enter the industry, and then it's gone.

376
01:76:01,640 --> 01:76:06,760
So if this occurs, in this case, the standard result

377
01:76:06,760 --> 01:76:10,680
is that the first mover does act like a monopolist after all.

378
01:76:10,680 --> 01:76:15,680
Because suppose I go and I burn my fixed costs.

379
01:76:15,680 --> 01:76:19,160
So I sink a cost, and I'm here.

380
01:76:19,160 --> 01:76:21,440
And at this point, I'm not going to leave just

381
01:76:21,440 --> 01:76:23,080
because I'm losing money.

382
01:76:23,080 --> 01:76:26,200
I'll only leave if the price falls below my marginal cost.

383
01:76:26,200 --> 01:76:28,700
And if an entrant knows this, then he says, wait a second,

384
01:76:28,700 --> 01:76:31,620
if I go in there and we compete, what will the two of us

385
01:76:31,620 --> 01:76:33,800
dry, or we will both sink our costs,

386
01:76:33,800 --> 01:76:35,040
drive the price down to?

387
01:76:35,040 --> 01:76:36,500
We'll drive down the marginal cost.

388
01:76:36,500 --> 01:76:39,320
So if I enter, this is not a way for me to steal his profit.

389
01:76:39,320 --> 01:76:43,080
This is a way for me to lose A. So I don't want to do this.

390
01:76:46,000 --> 01:76:49,880
So the pure strategy Nash equilibrium with sunk costs

391
01:76:49,880 --> 01:76:51,920
is, again, that the entrant comes and acts

392
01:76:51,920 --> 01:76:56,440
like a monopolist, and the entrant has no effect.

393
01:76:56,440 --> 01:77:00,880
But we can tweak this revised version

394
01:77:00,880 --> 01:77:02,800
and get back to a result that looks very much

395
01:77:02,800 --> 01:77:04,480
like an instability.

396
01:77:04,480 --> 01:77:07,040
What is the revised version?

397
01:77:07,040 --> 01:77:09,880
The revised version is that we look at the mixed strategy Nash

398
01:77:09,880 --> 01:77:10,380
equilibrium.

399
01:77:13,720 --> 01:77:15,560
And here, imagine there's two firms,

400
01:77:15,560 --> 01:77:17,920
and the first one says, you better not enter,

401
01:77:17,920 --> 01:77:19,760
because I'm going to go and pay a subcost.

402
01:77:19,760 --> 01:77:21,360
And then there'll be no point for you entering.

403
01:77:21,360 --> 01:77:22,680
You'll just lose money.

404
01:77:22,680 --> 01:77:24,960
And the other firm says, oh, really?

405
01:77:24,960 --> 01:77:27,760
That's interesting, because I'm going to do it first.

406
01:77:27,760 --> 01:77:29,200
And they say, oh, no, you won't.

407
01:77:29,200 --> 01:77:31,160
Oh, yes, I will.

408
01:77:31,160 --> 01:77:33,720
And you can imagine this argument saying, look.

409
01:77:33,720 --> 01:77:34,800
Like, look, I'm crazy.

410
01:77:34,800 --> 01:77:36,760
I do stuff like this all the time.

411
01:77:36,760 --> 01:77:38,640
Well, that's what you want people to believe.

412
01:77:38,640 --> 01:77:42,040
But I wasn't born yesterday, and I know you won't do it.

413
01:77:42,040 --> 01:77:45,280
So imagine this argument between two firms.

414
01:77:45,280 --> 01:77:47,680
Or, of course, it doesn't need to actually be an argument.

415
01:77:47,680 --> 01:77:50,000
It could be two entrepreneurs, neither of whom

416
01:77:50,000 --> 01:77:51,560
even knows of the other's existence,

417
01:77:51,560 --> 01:77:53,080
thinking, maybe I should enter.

418
01:77:53,080 --> 01:77:54,960
But then it could be just another guy like me

419
01:77:54,960 --> 01:77:56,760
spending money on entering, or he's already doing it.

420
01:77:56,760 --> 01:77:58,520
When I enter, I'm going to show up in the town.

421
01:77:58,520 --> 01:78:00,120
And then I'm just going to lose money.

422
01:78:00,120 --> 01:78:01,640
I'm going to be in the middle again.

423
01:78:01,640 --> 01:78:04,400
So it could be that.

424
01:78:04,400 --> 01:78:08,880
And so here we can go and set up the normal form.

425
01:78:08,880 --> 01:78:13,600
So firms can either be in or out, in or out.

426
01:78:13,600 --> 01:78:18,400
And if they both go in, then they both lose their subcost.

427
01:78:18,400 --> 01:78:21,520
So that's a disaster for them.

428
01:78:21,520 --> 01:78:23,100
And, of course, if they both stay out,

429
01:78:23,100 --> 01:78:25,600
they both get nothing, 0, 0.

430
01:78:25,600 --> 01:78:27,980
But then if one firm goes in and the other one goes out,

431
01:78:27,980 --> 01:78:30,240
the one that goes in gets the monopoly profit,

432
01:78:30,240 --> 01:78:32,280
and the other firm gets nothing.

433
01:78:32,280 --> 01:78:34,880
And if you stay out and your opponent goes in, you get 0,

434
01:78:34,880 --> 01:78:38,600
and your opponent gets monopoly profit.

435
01:78:38,600 --> 01:78:40,440
All right, now when you take a look at this,

436
01:78:40,440 --> 01:78:44,200
you'll see that we got two pure strategy Nash equilibrium.

437
01:78:44,200 --> 01:78:47,680
So is minus A minus A an equilibrium?

438
01:78:47,680 --> 01:78:49,760
No.

439
01:78:49,760 --> 01:78:51,920
Is the one where both are doing as badly as possible

440
01:78:51,920 --> 01:78:52,640
an equilibrium?

441
01:78:52,640 --> 01:78:53,960
No.

442
01:78:53,960 --> 01:78:56,280
No, so that's not an equilibrium.

443
01:78:56,280 --> 01:78:58,080
Is 0, 0 an equilibrium?

444
01:78:58,080 --> 01:78:58,580
No.

445
01:78:58,580 --> 01:79:01,880
No, because if no firm is there, then either firm

446
01:79:01,880 --> 01:79:04,480
could go and become the monopolist and wet there.

447
01:79:04,480 --> 01:79:06,840
And by the way, is this one good for customers?

448
01:79:06,840 --> 01:79:07,560
No.

449
01:79:07,560 --> 01:79:09,920
No, that was terrible for customers.

450
01:79:09,920 --> 01:79:12,280
So at least there's a monopolist getting rich out of us.

451
01:79:12,280 --> 01:79:14,080
Yeah, because there's no firm at all.

452
01:79:14,080 --> 01:79:16,160
So you don't get the product at all.

453
01:79:16,160 --> 01:79:17,680
And that's terrible.

454
01:79:17,680 --> 01:79:21,400
We'd rather have a monopoly supplier of food or no food.

455
01:79:21,400 --> 01:79:24,840
I, for one, prefer a monopoly to no food at all.

456
01:79:24,840 --> 01:79:26,560
But then if you look at these two,

457
01:79:26,560 --> 01:79:31,360
and these two are both going to be perfectly nice and fine pure

458
01:79:31,360 --> 01:79:33,760
strategy Nash equilibrium.

459
01:79:33,760 --> 01:79:35,640
Now on the homework, you're going

460
01:79:35,640 --> 01:79:39,120
to get to go and figure out whether there

461
01:79:39,120 --> 01:79:41,120
is a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium.

462
01:79:41,120 --> 01:79:43,160
Hint, there it is.

